window.GATE_BANK=window.GATE_BANK||{};window.GATE_BANK["computer-networks"]=[{"id": "CN-U1-Q01", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"1\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"osi-and-tcp-ip-models\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 1</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">osi-and-tcp-ip-models</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">In the OSI reference model, which layer is responsible for routing a packet from one network to another through intermediate routers?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Physical layer</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Data link layer</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Network layer</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Transport layer</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["Physical layer", "Data link layer", "Network layer", "Transport layer"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The OSI layers divide work by scope: the physical layer moves bits, the data link layer moves frames across a single link, the network layer moves packets across a sequence of links, and the transport layer moves data between processes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Routing means choosing the next hop for a packet whose source and destination lie in different networks.</li><li>That decision needs a global (network-wide) address, which is exactly what the network layer provides.</li><li>The data link layer only addresses the next machine on the same link; the transport layer does not see routers at all.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; the network layer.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Many candidates choose the transport layer because it is the first &lsquo;end-to-end&rsquo; layer. End-to-end delivery is transport; routing <i>inside</i> the network is network layer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Fix the vocabulary early: routing and logical addressing &rarr; network layer; framing and MAC addressing &rarr; data link; process-to-process delivery &rarr; transport.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q02", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"2\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"osi-and-tcp-ip-models\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 2</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">osi-and-tcp-ip-models</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following is NOT a layer of the TCP/IP reference model?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Application</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Transport</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Session</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Internet</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["Application", "Transport", "Session", "Internet"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The TCP/IP reference model has four layers &mdash; application, transport, internet and network access (host-to-network).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The application layer merges the OSI application, presentation and session functions.</li><li>Transport and internet are layers in their own right.</li><li>There is no separate session layer; session-style services (dialogue control, checkpointing) are either omitted or pushed into the application.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; Session.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Candidates who memorise &lsquo;seven layers&rsquo; often assume the session layer exists in every model. It is an OSI-only layer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> OSI = 7 layers (physical, data link, network, transport, session, presentation, application). TCP/IP = 4 (or 5 if the link layer is split into link and physical).</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q03", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"3\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"osi-and-tcp-ip-models\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 3</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">osi-and-tcp-ip-models</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A switch that forwards frames by looking up 48-bit MAC addresses operates at which OSI layer?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"3\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q3\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Physical layer</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"3\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q3\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Data link layer</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"3\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q3\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Network layer</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"3\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q3\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Transport layer</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["Physical layer", "Data link layer", "Network layer", "Transport layer"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Device class follows the header the device reads. A MAC address is a data link layer address, so a MAC-address switch is a layer-2 device.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The switch receives a frame, reads its destination MAC address, and forwards it out of the port learned for that address.</li><li>It does not modify or inspect the IP header, so it never reaches layer 3.</li><li>A hub, by contrast, only repeats electrical signals and is a layer-1 device; a router reads IP addresses and is a layer-3 device.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; the data link layer.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Switches are sometimes called &lsquo;layer-3 switches&rsquo; when they also route, which makes candidates assume all switches are layer 3. Plain MAC forwarding is layer 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Hub &rarr; layer 1, switch/bridge &rarr; layer 2, router &rarr; layer 3, and a device is named after the highest header it actually processes.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q04", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"4\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"osi-and-tcp-ip-models\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 4</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">osi-and-tcp-ip-models</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about layering and encapsulation are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">A router forwards a packet using the destination network-layer address and normally does not inspect the transport-layer header.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">The data link layer appends a trailer to the frame, while the network layer adds only a header.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">A hub regenerates and repeats bits, so it operates at the physical layer.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">A protocol converter that works at the application layer is, by definition, a layer-3 device.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["A router forwards a packet using the destination network-layer address and normally does not inspect the transport-layer header.", "The data link layer appends a trailer to the frame, while the network layer adds only a header.", "A hub regenerates and repeats bits, so it operates at the physical layer.", "A protocol converter that works at the application layer is, by definition, a layer-3 device."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each layer adds its own control information and reads only its own header; a router is a layer-3 relay and is transparent to transport headers.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; routing needs only the network-layer destination address; the transport header travels untouched.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; a data link frame carries a trailer (the frame check sequence); the network header has no trailer.</li><li>(C) True &mdash; a hub performs no frame parsing at all, so it is layer 1.</li><li>(D) False &mdash; an application-layer converter works at layer 7; a &lsquo;gateway&rsquo; in the general sense can operate at any layer.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (D) is the trap: the word &lsquo;gateway&rsquo; is sometimes used for a router (layer 3), but an application-level converter is a layer-7 device. The definition in (D) is simply wrong.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask of any claim &lsquo;which header does this entity have to read?&rsquo; &mdash; the answer fixes its layer.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q05", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"5\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"osi-and-tcp-ip-models\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 5</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">osi-and-tcp-ip-models</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements comparing the OSI and TCP/IP reference models are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"5\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q5\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">The TCP/IP application layer performs the functions that OSI splits across its session, presentation and application layers.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"5\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q5\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">The TCP/IP internet layer corresponds to the OSI network layer.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"5\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q5\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">The TCP/IP reference model has seven layers.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"5\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q5\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">The OSI model was designed before the protocols that implement it existed, whereas the TCP/IP model was written up after its protocols were already in use.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["The TCP/IP application layer performs the functions that OSI splits across its session, presentation and application layers.", "The TCP/IP internet layer corresponds to the OSI network layer.", "The TCP/IP reference model has seven layers.", "The OSI model was designed before the protocols that implement it existed, whereas the TCP/IP model was written up after its protocols were already in use."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> OSI is a protocol-independent architecture written first; TCP/IP is a description of an already-working protocol suite.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; presentation (encoding, compression, encryption) and session services are handled inside application protocols in TCP/IP.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; the internet layer provides logical addressing and routing, exactly the OSI network layer's job.</li><li>(C) False &mdash; TCP/IP has four layers (five if the link layer is split).</li><li>(D) True &mdash; the classic historical distinction: OSI came from a model, TCP/IP came from running code.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (C) is the usual error, produced by carrying the OSI count over to TCP/IP. Also note (D) is a genuine, examinable distinction, not trivia.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two facts to keep: TCP/IP = 4 layers and no session/presentation layer; OSI = 7 layers and model-first.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q06", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"6\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"osi-and-tcp-ip-models\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 6</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">osi-and-tcp-ip-models</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An application message of 600 bytes is passed down the stack. The transport layer adds a 20-byte header, the network layer adds a 20-byte header, and the data link layer adds a 14-byte header and a 4-byte trailer.<br/>What is the total size, in bytes, of the frame handed to the physical layer?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 6\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"6\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of bytes.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "658", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Encapsulation adds one header at each layer (and a trailer at the data link layer), so the sizes simply add.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Application data: 600 bytes.</li><li>Transport segment: 600 + 20 = 620 bytes.</li><li>Network datagram: 620 + 20 = 640 bytes.</li><li>Data link frame: 640 + 14 + 4 = 658 bytes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 658 bytes.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The common slip is to forget the 4-byte trailer, or to add the transport header twice because the segment is later called a payload. Overhead here is 58 bytes in total.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Layer overhead is cumulative: every header added below is carried by everything above it.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q07", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"7\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"osi-and-tcp-ip-models\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 7</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">osi-and-tcp-ip-models</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A frame travels from a source host to a destination host through two routers. Which statement about error checking on this path is correct?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"7\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q7\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Both the frame check sequence and the TCP checksum are verified only at the destination host.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"7\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q7\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">The frame check sequence is verified by every device that receives the frame, including each router; the TCP checksum is verified only at the destination host.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"7\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q7\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">The frame check sequence is verified only at the destination host; the TCP checksum is verified at every router.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"7\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q7\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Both the frame check sequence and the TCP checksum are verified at every router along the path.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["Both the frame check sequence and the TCP checksum are verified only at the destination host.", "The frame check sequence is verified by every device that receives the frame, including each router; the TCP checksum is verified only at the destination host.", "The frame check sequence is verified only at the destination host; the TCP checksum is verified at every router.", "Both the frame check sequence and the TCP checksum are verified at every router along the path."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Error detection is layered, and each layer's check has the scope of that layer: a data link check covers one hop, a transport check covers the whole end-to-end path.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>When a router receives a frame it recomputes the frame check sequence over the received bits; a bad frame is discarded on that hop.</li><li>The router then strips the data link header and trailer, so the next hop gets a brand-new frame with a newly computed check sequence.</li><li>The TCP header and its checksum travel end to end untouched, so only the destination transport layer can verify them &mdash; a router never even looks at them.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The tempting error is (D): students reason that &lsquo;every device checks for errors&rsquo;. A router cannot verify a TCP checksum without reassembling the transport segment, which it does not do.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Per-hop check &rArr; data link; end-to-end check &rArr; transport. This is the cleanest illustration of why both layers exist.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q08", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"8\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 8</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A 1500-byte packet is placed on a 1 Mbps link. Ignoring propagation, processing and queueing delay, what is the transmission delay?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">1.2 ms</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">12 ms</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">120 ms</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">0.12 ms</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["1.2 ms", "12 ms", "120 ms", "0.12 ms"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Transmission delay is the time to push all the bits of the packet onto the link: <code>L / R</code>, with L in bits and R in bits per second.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>L = 1500 bytes = 1500 &times; 8 = 12000 bits.</li><li>R = 1 Mbps = 10&#8310; bits per second.</li><li>L / R = 12000 / 10&#8310; = 0.012 s = 12 ms.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 12 ms.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the factor of 8 (bytes to bits) gives 1.5 ms or 1.2 ms, which is why those distractors are present.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Always convert to bits before dividing by a bit rate: <code>delay = 8 &times; size_in_bytes / bandwidth</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q09", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"9\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 9</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Two links have the same length and the same propagation speed, but link 1 has twice the bandwidth of link 2. For the same packet, which of the following is TRUE?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"9\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q9\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">The propagation delay is the same on both links, and the transmission delay is smaller on link 1.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"9\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q9\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Both delays are smaller on link 1.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"9\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q9\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">The transmission delay is the same on both links, and the propagation delay is smaller on link 1.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"9\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q9\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Both delays are the same on both links.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["The propagation delay is the same on both links, and the transmission delay is smaller on link 1.", "Both delays are smaller on link 1.", "The transmission delay is the same on both links, and the propagation delay is smaller on link 1.", "Both delays are the same on both links."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Transmission delay depends on the link's bandwidth; propagation delay depends only on distance and signal speed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Propagation delay = distance / propagation speed, and both are identical, so the two links have equal propagation delay.</li><li>Transmission delay = L / R; R is doubled on link 1, so its transmission delay is halved.</li><li>Therefore only the transmission delay changes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The frequent error is (B): assuming a &lsquo;faster link&rsquo; shortens everything. Bandwidth cannot make a signal travel faster than the propagation speed of the medium.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Bandwidth governs how long the packet takes to enter the wire; distance governs how long it takes to cross it.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q10", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"10\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 10</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A link has a bandwidth of 100 Mbps and a one-way propagation delay of 20 ms.<br/>What is the bandwidth-delay product of the link, in kilobytes? Use 1 kB = 1000 bytes and enter a number.</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 10\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"10\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: a number in kB (1 kB = 1000 bytes).</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "250", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bandwidth-delay product = bandwidth &times; propagation delay. It is the number of bits that can be in flight on the link at one instant.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>R = 100 Mbps = 10&#8312; bits/s; delay = 20 ms = 0.02 s.</li><li>BDP = 10&#8312; &times; 0.02 = 2 &times; 10&#8310; bits.</li><li>In bytes: 2 &times; 10&#8310; / 8 = 250000 bytes.</li><li>In kB (1000 bytes): 250000 / 1000 = 250 kB.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 250 kB.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two errors dominate: dividing by 1024 instead of 1000, and using bits per byte wrongly, giving 244 or 2000. The question fixes 1 kB = 1000 bytes to remove the first ambiguity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> BDP answers &lsquo;how much data must be outstanding to keep the pipe full?&rsquo; &mdash; the basis of the TCP window sizing question.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q11", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"11\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 11</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Two hosts are 3000 km apart on a link whose signal propagates at 3 &times; 10&#8312; m/s. What is the one-way propagation delay?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">1 ms</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">10 ms</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">100 ms</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">0.1 ms</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["1 ms", "10 ms", "100 ms", "0.1 ms"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Propagation delay = distance / propagation speed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Distance = 3000 km = 3 &times; 10&#8310; m.</li><li>Speed = 3 &times; 10&#8312; m/s.</li><li>Delay = 3 &times; 10&#8310; / 3 &times; 10&#8312; = 10&#8315;&#178; s = 10 ms.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 10 ms.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A kilometre-to-metre slip (using 3000 m) gives 0.01 ms, and a factor-of-ten slip gives 100 ms. Both appear as options.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Keep units consistent: convert km to m before dividing by m/s, and remember 10&#8315;&#179; s = 1 ms.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q12", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"12\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 12</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about delay, bandwidth and the bandwidth-delay product are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"12\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q12\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">The bandwidth-delay product of a link equals the maximum number of bits that can be in transit on that link at any instant.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"12\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q12\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Doubling the propagation delay and halving the bandwidth leaves the bandwidth-delay product unchanged.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"12\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q12\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">The transmission delay of a packet depends on the distance between the two end hosts.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"12\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q12\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">The throughput of a path is limited by the slowest link on it, while the end-to-end delay is the sum of the per-link delays.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["The bandwidth-delay product of a link equals the maximum number of bits that can be in transit on that link at any instant.", "Doubling the propagation delay and halving the bandwidth leaves the bandwidth-delay product unchanged.", "The transmission delay of a packet depends on the distance between the two end hosts.", "The throughput of a path is limited by the slowest link on it, while the end-to-end delay is the sum of the per-link delays."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> BDP = R &times; T<sub>prop</sub> is the &lsquo;amount of pipe&rsquo;; transmission delay is L / R; throughput of a path is set by the bottleneck link.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; that is the definition: the bits that fit inside the link while the first bit is still travelling.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; (R/2) &times; (2T) = R &times; T.</li><li>(C) False &mdash; transmission delay is L / R and contains no distance term; it is the propagation delay that depends on distance.</li><li>(D) True &mdash; for a single flow the sustained rate cannot exceed the minimum link rate, and the delays add up hop by hop.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (C) is the standard confusion between the two delays. A satellite link of huge length but high bandwidth still has a tiny transmission delay and a large propagation delay.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Transmission delay: packet size vs bandwidth. Propagation delay: distance vs signal speed. Never mix them.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q13", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"13\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 13</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A 1000-byte packet is sent from a source to a destination over three links in series, each of bandwidth 1 Mbps. The switches are store-and-forward and propagation delay is negligible. What is the total time from the first bit leaving the source to the last bit arriving at the destination?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">8 ms</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">16 ms</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">24 ms</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">32 ms</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["8 ms", "16 ms", "24 ms", "32 ms"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Under store-and-forward, a switch must receive the whole packet before it can begin transmitting it on the next link, so each hop costs one full transmission time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One transmission time = 1000 &times; 8 / 10&#8310; = 8 ms.</li><li>The source pushes the packet onto link 1 in 8 ms; the packet then needs 8 ms on link 2 and 8 ms on link 3.</li><li>Total = 3 &times; 8 = 24 ms.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 24 ms.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The popular wrong answer is 8 ms, from imagining that the bits flow continuously through all three links at once. That is cut-through switching, not store-and-forward.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Store-and-forward over k equal links: total = k &times; L/R (plus propagation). Only the first hop overlaps with nothing.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q14", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "framing-bit-byte-stuffing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"14\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"framing-bit-byte-stuffing\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 14</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">framing-bit-byte-stuffing</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Why is bit stuffing used in a bit-oriented framing protocol such as HDLC?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"14\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q14\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">To make sure the flag pattern 01111110 never appears inside the data.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"14\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q14\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">To detect bit errors introduced by the channel.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"14\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q14\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">To compress the data before transmission.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"14\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q14\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">To keep the receiver's clock synchronised with the sender's.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["To make sure the flag pattern 01111110 never appears inside the data.", "To detect bit errors introduced by the channel.", "To compress the data before transmission.", "To keep the receiver's clock synchronised with the sender's."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Framing needs an unambiguous delimiter. If the delimiter could occur in the payload, the receiver could not tell data from a frame boundary.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>HDLC uses the flag 01111110 at the start and end of every frame.</li><li>A payload containing six consecutive 1s would imitate that flag.</li><li>The sender therefore inserts a 0 after every five consecutive 1s, so six 1s can never occur inside a frame.</li><li>The receiver deletes the inserted 0 again.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; transparency of the flag.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Bit stuffing is often confused with error detection (option B). HDLC detects errors with the frame check sequence; stuffing only guarantees frame transparency.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Framing problems are solved by transparency mechanisms (stuffing, escaping), never by checksums.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q15", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "framing-bit-byte-stuffing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"15\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"framing-bit-byte-stuffing\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 15</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">framing-bit-byte-stuffing</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">In byte-oriented framing, FLAG = 0x7E and ESC = 0x7D, and the escape rule is to transmit ESC followed by the data byte XORed with 0x20. How is a data byte 0x7D transmitted?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">0x7D 0x7D</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">0x7D 0x5D</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">0x5D</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">0x7E 0x7D</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["0x7D 0x7D", "0x7D 0x5D", "0x5D", "0x7E 0x7D"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every occurrence of the flag byte and of the escape byte itself must be escaped, so the receiver never mistakes data for a delimiter.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The data byte is 0x7D, which equals ESC.</li><li>XOR with 0x20: 0x7D &oplus; 0x20 = 0x5D.</li><li>Transmit ESC first, then the escaped byte: 0x7D 0x5D.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 0x7D 0x5D.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Many candidates escape only the flag. Forgetting that the escape byte itself must be escaped makes the receiver treat a data 0x7D as the start of an escape sequence.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Two bytes need escaping in byte stuffing: the flag and the escape byte itself. The XOR with 0x20 flips bit 5, which maps 0x7E&rarr;0x5E and 0x7D&rarr;0x5D.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q16", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "framing-bit-byte-stuffing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"16\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"framing-bit-byte-stuffing\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 16</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">framing-bit-byte-stuffing</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A frame uses byte stuffing with FLAG = 0x7E and ESC = 0x7D; an escape sequence is ESC followed by the data byte XORed with 0x20. The data to be transmitted is the six-byte sequence<br/><code>41 7E 7D 42 7E 43</code> (hex).<br/>How many bytes are transmitted between the two flags?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 16\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"16\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of bytes.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "9", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Byte stuffing replaces each occurrence of FLAG or ESC by a two-byte escape sequence; every other byte passes through unchanged.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>0x41 is neither FLAG nor ESC &rarr; 0x41 (1 byte).</li><li>0x7E = FLAG &rarr; 0x7D 0x5E (2 bytes).</li><li>0x7D = ESC &rarr; 0x7D 0x5D (2 bytes).</li><li>0x42 &rarr; 0x42 (1 byte).</li><li>0x7E &rarr; 0x7D 0x5E (2 bytes).</li><li>0x43 &rarr; 0x43 (1 byte).</li><li>Total = 1 + 2 + 2 + 1 + 2 + 1 = 9 bytes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 9 bytes.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The usual mistakes are escaping only the two FLAG bytes (giving 8) or forgetting that the ESC byte must itself be escaped (giving 8 as well, but for a different reason).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count the growth: each escaped byte adds exactly one byte to the frame, so the payload grows from 6 to 9.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q17", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "framing-bit-byte-stuffing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"17\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"framing-bit-byte-stuffing\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 17</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">framing-bit-byte-stuffing</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">The data bit stream <code>011111101111101111110</code> is to be transmitted with bit stuffing, where a 0 is inserted after every five consecutive 1s. How many bits are inserted?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">2</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">3</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">4</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">5</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["2", "3", "4", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Scan the stream keeping a running count of consecutive 1s; whenever the count reaches five, insert a 0 and reset the counter.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Group 1: the leading <code>0111111</code> has six consecutive 1s &rarr; one 0 inserted after the fifth 1.</li><li>The inserted 0 resets the run, so the sixth 1 starts a new run of length 1.</li><li>Group 2: <code>11111</code> &rarr; one 0 inserted.</li><li>Group 3: <code>111111</code> &rarr; one 0 inserted after the fifth 1.</li><li>Total insertions = 3.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 3 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The trap is to treat a run of six 1s as a single insertion of two 0s, or to stop counting once a 0 has been inserted. Each insertion resets the counter, and each new run of five earns its own insertion.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Work left to right with a counter, never by eye: after inserting a 0 the run length is 0, and the next bit restarts it.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q18", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "framing-bit-byte-stuffing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"18\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"framing-bit-byte-stuffing\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 18</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">framing-bit-byte-stuffing</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about framing techniques are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"18\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q18\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">In byte-oriented framing, only the flag byte and the escape byte itself need to be escaped.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"18\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q18\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">In bit-oriented framing, the receiver removes a 0 that immediately follows five consecutive 1s.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"18\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q18\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Character-count framing keeps synchronisation even if the count field of a frame is corrupted.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"18\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q18\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">In character-count framing, a corrupted count field destroys the framing of all the following frames in the stream.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["In byte-oriented framing, only the flag byte and the escape byte itself need to be escaped.", "In bit-oriented framing, the receiver removes a 0 that immediately follows five consecutive 1s.", "Character-count framing keeps synchronisation even if the count field of a frame is corrupted.", "In character-count framing, a corrupted count field destroys the framing of all the following frames in the stream."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> There are three standard framing methods: character count, flag bytes with stuffing, and flag bits with bit stuffing. Each has a different failure mode.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; any other byte value can appear in the data without ambiguity, so only FLAG and ESC need escaping.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; that is exactly the de-stuffing rule, and it is what makes six 1s impossible inside a frame.</li><li>(C) False and (D) True &mdash; the count field tells the receiver where the frame ends, so a corrupted count shifts every subsequent boundary. This is the classic weakness of character-count framing.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statements (C) and (D) are contradictory, so one must be false: candidates who pick both have not modelled what the receiver does with the count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Character count: simple, but one bad count desynchronises the stream. Flag-based methods recover because the receiver can rescan for the next flag.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q19", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "framing-bit-byte-stuffing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"19\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"framing-bit-byte-stuffing\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 19</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">framing-bit-byte-stuffing</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">The data stream <code>01111101111110</code> is transmitted with bit stuffing (a 0 is inserted after every five consecutive 1s). Which is the transmitted stream?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">0111110111111010</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">01111100111111010</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">0111110011111010</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">011111001111010</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["0111110111111010", "01111100111111010", "0111110011111010", "011111001111010"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bit stuffing is applied left to right with a counter of consecutive 1s; each insertion adds a 0 and resets the counter.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Original: <code>0 1 1 1 1 1 0 1 1 1 1 1 1 0</code> (14 bits).</li><li>Bits 2&ndash;6 are five 1s &rarr; insert 0 after bit 6.</li><li>Bit 7 is 0; then bits 8&ndash;12 are five 1s &rarr; insert 0 after bit 12.</li><li>Bit 13 is 1 (a new run of length 1) and bit 14 is 0, so no further insertion.</li><li>Result: <code>0 1 1 1 1 1 0 0 1 1 1 1 1 0 1 0</code> = <code>0111110011111010</code> (16 bits).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; <code>0111110011111010</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (A) is the original stream with two 0s appended, and option (B) has 17 bits because a 0 was inserted after a run of only four 1s. Both come from stuffing by eye rather than by counter.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Write the output bit by bit and keep the counter visible; the length grows by exactly the number of insertions.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q20", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"20\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 20</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">What is the Hamming distance between the two 7-bit codewords 1011101 and 1001001?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"20\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q20\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">1</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"20\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q20\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">2</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"20\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q20\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">3</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"20\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q20\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">4</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The Hamming distance is the number of bit positions in which the two codewords differ.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Align the words: <code>1 0 1 1 1 0 1</code> and <code>1 0 0 1 0 0 1</code>.</li><li>Position 1: 1 = 1. Position 2: 0 = 0. Position 3: 1 &ne; 0. Position 4: 1 = 1.</li><li>Position 5: 1 &ne; 0. Position 6: 0 = 0. Position 7: 1 = 1.</li><li>Two positions differ, so the distance is 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The slip is to compare the decimal values or to count matching positions instead of differing ones.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Distance = number of differing positions = weight of the XOR of the two words: <code>1011101 &oplus; 1001001 = 0010100</code>, which has weight 2.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q21", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"21\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 21</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A block code has minimum Hamming distance 5. How many bit errors can it (i) detect and (ii) correct?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Detect 4, correct 2</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Detect 2, correct 4</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Detect 5, correct 2</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Detect 2, correct 1</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["Detect 4, correct 2", "Detect 2, correct 4", "Detect 5, correct 2", "Detect 2, correct 1"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With minimum distance d: detection capability is d &minus; 1 errors, correction capability is &lfloor;(d &minus; 1)/2&rfloor; errors.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>d = 5, so up to d &minus; 1 = 4 errors can be detected.</li><li>Correction: &lfloor;4/2&rfloor; = 2 errors.</li><li>Intuition: a received word with 2 errors is still strictly nearer its own codeword than any other (distance 2 against at least 3).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; detect 4, correct 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Candidates often answer &lsquo;detect 5&rsquo;. With 5 errors a word can land exactly on another codeword, so 5 errors are not detectable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Memorise the pair: detect &le; d&minus;1, correct &le; &lfloor;(d&minus;1)/2&rfloor;. Every error-detection question reduces to these two.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q22", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"22\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 22</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A single-error-correcting Hamming code is to be built for 16 data bits.<br/>What is the minimum number of parity bits required?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 22\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"22\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of parity bits.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With m data bits and r parity bits, the r check positions must be able to address every one of the m + r positions plus the &lsquo;no error&rsquo; case: m + r + 1 &le; 2<sup>r</sup>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Try r = 4: 16 + 4 + 1 = 21 &gt; 16 = 2&#8308;, so 4 parity bits cannot address all error patterns.</li><li>Try r = 5: 16 + 5 + 1 = 22 &le; 32 = 2&#8309;, which works.</li><li>So the minimum is 5 parity bits, giving a 21-bit codeword.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 5 parity bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The common error is to use 2<sup>r</sup> &ge; m only, which gives 4. The parity bits also occupy positions that must be addressable, and the all-zero syndrome must be reserved for &lsquo;no error&rsquo;.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For Hamming codes, solve the smallest r with m + r + 1 &le; 2<sup>r</sup>. For m = 4, 7, 8, 16 the answers are 3, 4, 4, 5.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q23", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"23\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 23</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about CRC and checksums are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"23\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q23\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">A CRC whose generator polynomial has at least two non-zero terms detects every single-bit error.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"23\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q23\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">A CRC with a generator polynomial of degree r detects every burst error of length at most r.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"23\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q23\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">If the CRC remainder is zero, the received frame is guaranteed to be error-free.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"23\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q23\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">CRC arithmetic is polynomial division over GF(2), where addition and subtraction are both the XOR operation.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["A CRC whose generator polynomial has at least two non-zero terms detects every single-bit error.", "A CRC with a generator polynomial of degree r detects every burst error of length at most r.", "If the CRC remainder is zero, the received frame is guaranteed to be error-free.", "CRC arithmetic is polynomial division over GF(2), where addition and subtraction are both the XOR operation."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CRC treats the frame as a polynomial over GF(2); the properties follow from divisibility by the generator polynomial g(x).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; a single-bit error is x<sup>i</sup>, and g(x) with two or more terms cannot divide a monomial.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; a burst of length &le; r has degree below r, so it cannot be a non-zero multiple of g(x).</li><li>(C) False &mdash; a zero remainder only means the received polynomial is divisible by g(x); an error pattern that is itself a multiple of g(x) escapes detection.</li><li>(D) True &mdash; coefficients are in GF(2), so +1 = &minus;1 and both operations are XOR.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (C) is the classic over-claim. CRC is a detection scheme with a small, bounded probability of a missed error, not a guarantee &mdash; which is exactly why (D)'s algebraic view matters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Learn the two guarantees (all single-bit errors; all bursts of length &le; r) and the one non-guarantee (no error-free certification).</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q24", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"24\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 24</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A datagram header is to be protected by the 16-bit Internet checksum. The three 16-bit words to be summed are 0x1234, 0x5678 and 0x9ABC. Using one's complement arithmetic, what is the checksum field value?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">0x0369</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">0xFC96</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">0xFC95</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">0xFC69</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["0x0369", "0xFC96", "0xFC95", "0xFC69"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The Internet checksum adds the 16-bit words in one's complement arithmetic (end-around carry), then takes the one's complement of the result.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>0x1234 + 0x5678 = 0x68AC.</li><li>0x68AC + 0x9ABC = 0x10368, which overflows 16 bits.</li><li>End-around carry: 0x0368 + 1 = 0x0369.</li><li>Checksum = one's complement of 0x0369 = 0xFFFF &minus; 0x0369 = 0xFC96.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 0xFC96.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dropping the end-around carry gives 0xFC95, and forgetting the complement gives 0x0369; both are offered as options. Note that 0xFC96 is not the arithmetic negation in two's complement either.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Checksum routine: sum with wraparound, fold the carry in, then complement. Verify by adding the checksum back &mdash; the total must be 0xFFFF.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q25", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"25\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"network-fundamentals-framing\" data-unit-number=\"1\" data-subtopic=\"error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 25</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A CRC is computed with the generator polynomial x&#179; + 1, whose bit pattern is <code>1001</code>. The message bits are <code>1101</code> and three zeros are appended before division.<br/>Enter the decimal value of the resulting 3-bit remainder.</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 25\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"25\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer, the decimal value of the 3-bit remainder.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CRC division is long division over GF(2): align the divisor under the leading 1 of the current remainder and XOR, with no borrows or carries.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Append three zeros: <code>1101000</code>.</li><li>Leading 4 bits 1101 &oplus; 1001 = 0100; bring down 0 &rarr; 1000; 1000 &oplus; 1001 = 0001.</li><li>Bring down the next 0 &rarr; 0010; the leading bit is 0, so XOR nothing. Bring down the last 0 &rarr; 0100; again nothing.</li><li>The 3-bit remainder is <code>100</code>, i.e. 4 in decimal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 (remainder <code>100</code>).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The trap is ordinary binary subtraction with borrows. In GF(2) there is no borrow: XOR is the only operation, so a step where the leading bit is 0 just shifts down the next bit.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a degree-r generator the remainder is always r bits; read it as a plain binary number if the answer must be decimal.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q26", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which OSI layer is responsible for data compression and for converting between different data representations such as ASCII and EBCDIC?", "options": ["Session layer", "Application layer", "Data link layer", "Presentation layer"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The OSI model separates user-facing work from syntax work. The application layer offers the service the user asks for, the presentation layer fixes the <i>form</i> of the data (compression, encryption, character-set conversion), and the session layer manages the dialogue between two applications.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Compression removes redundancy from a representation; it changes how the data is encoded, not what service is requested.</li><li>Code conversion between ASCII and EBCDIC is the same kind of job: it rewrites the syntax of the data.</li><li>Both are exactly the functions the presentation layer owns.</li><li>The session layer manages dialogue control (checkpoints, synchronisation) and the application layer handles the user service itself.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; the presentation layer.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Encryption and compression are often attributed to the application layer because an application often requests them. In the OSI reference model they belong to presentation, which is exactly the layer the TCP/IP model does not have.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask what is being changed: the service (application), the form of the data (presentation), or the dialogue (session).</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q27", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The protocol data units (PDUs) of the transport, network and data link layers are, in that order, called:", "options": ["frame, packet, segment", "segment, datagram, frame", "datagram, segment, frame", "segment, frame, datagram"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each layer names the unit it hands down: the transport layer produces a segment, the network layer a datagram (packet), and the data link layer a frame; the physical layer moves raw bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The transport layer takes the byte stream and adds its header, producing a segment.</li><li>The network layer wraps the segment in an IP header, producing a packet or datagram.</li><li>The data link layer wraps the datagram with a header and a trailer, producing a frame.</li><li>So the order transport &rarr; network &rarr; data link gives segment &rarr; datagram &rarr; frame.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; segment, datagram, frame.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The pairs are easy to rotate. The trap is placing the frame before the packet; a frame is the outermost encapsulation, so it is produced last on the way down and removed first on the way up.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Anchor the order by encapsulation depth: segment inside datagram inside frame.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q28", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Flow control and error control appear at more than one layer. Which statement is correct?", "options": ["The data link layer controls flow and errors between two adjacent nodes on one link, while the transport layer controls them between the two end processes.", "Only the transport layer performs flow control; the data link layer has none.", "The network layer performs end-to-end flow control between processes.", "Flow control in the data link layer protects against errors introduced inside routers."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> OSI draws a scope line: the data link layer works link by link between adjacent nodes, the transport layer works end to end between processes. Both perform flow and error control, but over different spans.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A frame exists only on one link, so its acknowledgement and window can only concern that link &mdash; that is link-by-link control.</li><li>A segment survives every hop, so its window and acknowledgement concern the two end processes &mdash; that is end-to-end control.</li><li>The network layer forwards packets; it does not run a window between processes.</li><li>Errors introduced inside a router are exactly what the end-to-end transport checksum is there to catch, not the link-layer window.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Candidates read &lsquo;flow control&rsquo; as a single function belonging to one layer. It is a family of mechanisms, and the same name at two layers means two different scopes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Name the scope before naming the layer: adjacent nodes &rarr; data link, end processes &rarr; transport.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q29", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A router forwards an IPv4 packet that arrives on one Ethernet and leaves on another. Ignoring NAT, which of the following are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The source and destination MAC addresses of the outgoing frame differ from those of the incoming frame.", "The source and destination IPv4 addresses in the packet are unchanged.", "The TTL field is decremented by one.", "The transport-layer checksum must be recomputed by the router because the frame addresses changed."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A router terminates the link layer on each interface and forwards at the network layer, so link-layer addresses are local to a link while network-layer addresses are end to end.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>MAC addresses identify a network card on one link. The outgoing frame is built for the next hop, so both MAC addresses are rewritten.</li><li>The IPv4 addresses name the end hosts and are carried unchanged through every router (NAT aside).</li><li>The TTL is decremented at each router so that packets cannot loop forever.</li><li>The transport checksum covers a pseudo-header of IP addresses and the transport payload; neither changes here, so the router does not recompute it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> &lsquo;The router changes the addresses, so it must fix the checksum&rsquo; conflates the two address spaces. Only the MAC addresses change, and no transport checksum covers them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Per hop: MAC addresses and TTL change. End to end: IP addresses and the transport checksum survive.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q30", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A router forwards IPv4 packets between its attached links. It implements the physical and data link layers on each link and the network layer for forwarding; it runs no transport-layer or application-layer protocol of its own. How many of the seven OSI layers does this router implement?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of OSI layers a device implements is the number of distinct layers whose functions it actually performs, not the number of headers it happens to read.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Physical layer: it drives the line and moves bits on each interface.</li><li>Data link layer: it frames and unframes, checks the frame check sequence and addresses the next hop.</li><li>Network layer: it runs the routing table and forwards the packet.</li><li>It runs no transport or application protocol, and OSI session and presentation functions are absent entirely, so the count stops at three.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 layers.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting all seven because the router &lsquo;sees&rsquo; the whole packet. Reading a header is not implementing a layer; the router never processes the transport header at all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count the layers whose functions the device performs, and stop where its processing stops.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q31", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "osi-and-tcp-ip-models", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Every link uses a frame check sequence that each router verifies and recomputes. Why does TCP nevertheless carry its own checksum?", "options": ["Because a frame check sequence cannot detect burst errors.", "Because a packet can be corrupted inside a router, after its incoming frame check sequence has been verified, and the outgoing link will then protect the corrupted packet with a fresh, valid frame check sequence.", "Because the frame check sequence covers only the frame header.", "Because routers strip the frame check sequence before forwarding the frame."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A hop-by-hop check protects a hop; it cannot protect the path. The end-to-end argument places a correctness check at the endpoints precisely because intermediate systems can introduce errors of their own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The incoming frame check sequence is verified when the frame arrives, so it says nothing about anything that happens afterwards.</li><li>Inside a router the packet is held in memory, moved across a switching fabric and rewritten; any of these can corrupt it.</li><li>The outgoing frame gets a newly computed check sequence over the corrupted bits, which therefore validates perfectly at the next hop.</li><li>Only a check computed at the source and verified at the destination spans the whole path, which is what the TCP checksum provides.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The tempting answer is that the link check is somehow weak. It is strong; it is simply local. A correct hop check on a packet corrupted inside a router is a false reassurance, and that gap is exactly what the transport checksum closes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A check protects the span over which it is computed and verified; hop-by-hop protection leaves the router itself uncovered.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q32", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which component of nodal delay grows without bound as the traffic intensity La/R approaches 1?", "options": ["Transmission delay", "Propagation delay", "Queueing delay", "Processing delay"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Nodal delay is the sum of processing, queueing, transmission and propagation delay. Only queueing delay depends on how busy the link is.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Transmission delay is <code>L/R</code>, fixed by packet size and link rate.</li><li>Propagation delay is <code>d/s</code>, fixed by distance and signal speed.</li><li>Processing delay is a small fixed cost per packet.</li><li>Queueing delay is the time a packet waits behind others; as the arrival rate approaches the service rate the backlog, and hence the wait, diverges.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; queueing delay.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code>La/R</code> as a transmission-delay formula. The product <code>La</code> is the bit arrival rate and <code>R</code> the bit service rate, so the ratio measures load, not transmission time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Only queueing delay is a function of traffic; the other three are properties of the packet, the link and the distance.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q33", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Packets of 1500 bytes arrive at a link of rate 1 Mbps at an average rate of 60 packets per second. What is the traffic intensity La/R?", "options": ["0.72", "0.072", "7.2", "72"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Traffic intensity is the ratio of the bit arrival rate <code>La</code> to the bit service rate <code>R</code>; below 1 the queue is stable, above 1 it grows without bound.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Packet size <code>L</code> = 1500 bytes = 1500 &times; 8 = 12000 bits.</li><li>Arrival rate <code>a</code> = 60 packets per second, so the bit arrival rate is 60 &times; 12000 = 720000 bits/s.</li><li>Link rate <code>R</code> = 1 Mbps = 1000000 bits/s.</li><li>Traffic intensity = 720000 / 1000000 = 0.72.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 0.72 &mdash; option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using 1500 instead of 12000, which mixes bytes with bits and gives 0.072. The link rate is in bits per second, so the packet size must be converted to bits first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Convert to bits once, at the start, and the ratio is dimensionless.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q34", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A link has a bandwidth of 10 Mbps and a one-way propagation delay of 25 ms. A sender keeps the link fully utilised with a sliding window. Ignoring header overhead and acknowledgements, what is the minimum window size in bits?", "options": [], "answer_display": "250000", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A sender can have at most one bandwidth-delay product of data in flight; the window must be at least that large to keep the pipe full.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The bandwidth-delay product is <code>R &times; d<sub>prop</sub></code>.</li><li><code>R</code> = 10 Mbps = 10<sup>7</sup> bits/s.</li><li><code>d<sub>prop</sub></code> = 25 ms = 0.025 s.</li><li>Window = 10<sup>7</sup> &times; 0.025 = 250000 bits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 250000 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the round-trip time. The window must cover the bits that can be in the pipe in one direction; doubling to the RTT overstates the requirement by a factor of two.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Window for full utilisation = bandwidth &times; one-way propagation delay, in matching units.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q35", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "N packets, each of L bits, are sent back to back from a source to a destination over a path of k store-and-forward links, each of bandwidth R. Propagation, processing and queueing delays are negligible. What is the time from the instant the first bit leaves the source until the last bit reaches the destination?", "options": ["(k + N)L/R", "(k + N − 1)L/R", "kNL/R", "(k + N − 1)R/L"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With store-and-forward, the first packet pays one transmission delay on every link; each of the remaining N &minus; 1 packets then emerges one transmission delay after the previous one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first packet must be fully received and retransmitted at each of the <code>k</code> links: time <code>kL/R</code>.</li><li>After that the pipeline is full, and every further packet appears at the destination <code>L/R</code> later.</li><li>There are <code>N &minus; 1</code> further packets, contributing <code>(N &minus; 1)L/R</code>.</li><li>Total = <code>kL/R + (N &minus; 1)L/R = (k + N &minus; 1)L/R</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; <code>(k + N &minus; 1)L/R</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Multiplying to get <code>kNL/R</code> treats the links as serial rather than pipelined, which is what a store-and-forward chain does <i>not</i> do once packets are flowing. Option (A) forgets that the last packet needs no extra link time after it has traversed all k links.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count the first packet's full traversal, then add one transmission delay per remaining packet.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q36", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about delay and throughput are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The throughput of an end-to-end path cannot exceed the bandwidth of its bottleneck link.", "Propagation delay increases with the size of the packet.", "For a fixed packet size and arrival rate, increasing the link bandwidth reduces the traffic intensity.", "In the ideal queueing model, once the traffic intensity exceeds 1 the queue at a store-and-forward router grows without bound."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Throughput is set by the slowest link on the path; traffic intensity is <code>La/R</code>, so raising <code>R</code> lowers it; and a queue is stable only while the arrival rate stays below the service rate.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The bottleneck link must carry every bit of the flow, so it caps the end-to-end rate: (A) is true.</li><li>Propagation delay is <code>d/s</code>, a property of distance and signal speed; it does not see the packet at all: (B) is false.</li><li><code>La/R</code> falls as <code>R</code> rises with <code>L</code> and <code>a</code> fixed: (C) is true.</li><li>With arrival rate above service rate the backlog increases by a positive amount per unit time, so the queue length diverges: (D) is true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is the standard error: a longer packet spends longer <i>on the wire</i>, which is transmission delay, not propagation delay. The bit at the head of the packet still crosses the link in <code>d/s</code> seconds.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Transmission delay belongs to the packet and the rate; propagation delay belongs to the wire.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q37", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "transmission-delays-bandwidth-delay-product", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A 500-byte packet is sent over a 1 Mbps link that is 1000 km long; the signal propagates at 2 × 108 m/s. Ignoring queueing and processing, what is the one-way delay?", "options": ["4 ms", "5 ms", "9 ms", "20 ms"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> One-way delay is the sum of transmission delay <code>L/R</code> and propagation delay <code>d/s</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>L</code> = 500 bytes = 4000 bits, so transmission delay = 4000 / 10<sup>6</sup> = 4 ms.</li><li><code>d</code> = 1000 km = 10<sup>6</sup> m, so propagation delay = 10<sup>6</sup> / (2 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) = 5 ms.</li><li>The two are independent and additive: 4 + 5 = 9 ms.</li><li>Neither queueing nor processing delay is present, so nothing else is added.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 9 ms &mdash; option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 4 ms or 5 ms, one component only. The other classic slip is subtracting instead of adding because the two delays feel like alternatives; they occur one after the other on a single link.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Put the packet on the wire (L/R), then let the last bit travel (d/s): add the two.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q38", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "framing-bit-byte-stuffing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In character-count framing, the count field of a frame states:", "options": ["the number of characters in the data field only", "the number of frames that follow it in the stream", "the number of bits in the frame", "the number of characters in the whole frame, including the count field itself"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Character-count framing prefixes each frame with a field giving the length of that frame in characters, counted from the count field itself to the end of the frame.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The receiver reads the count field first and then consumes exactly that many characters to reach the end of the frame.</li><li>The count must therefore include the count field, or the receiver would stop short by the width of that field.</li><li>It is a character count, not a bit count, and it describes this frame only.</li><li>Everything after those characters is the start of the next frame.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the count as the payload length. If it excluded the header the receiver would lose synchronisation after the very first frame, which is the same failure mode as a corrupted count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The count spans the frame it heads, count field included.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q39", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "framing-bit-byte-stuffing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A data stream of 1024 bits is transmitted with bit stuffing, where a 0 is inserted after every five consecutive 1s. What is the largest number of bits that can be inserted for this stream?", "options": [], "answer_display": "204", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bit stuffing inserts one 0 for every completed run of five 1s; the number inserted is therefore maximised when the data is all 1s.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take the worst case: all 1024 data bits are 1.</li><li>Insertions happen after bits 5, 10, 15, &hellip;, that is after every multiple of 5.</li><li>The number of multiples of 5 not exceeding 1024 is &lfloor;1024 / 5&rfloor; = 204.</li><li>The remaining 1024 &minus; 5 &times; 204 = 4 trailing 1s do not complete another run of five, so nothing further is inserted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 204 inserted bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rounding up to 205. The final partial run of four 1s never reaches five, so no sixth insertion can occur; a stuffed stream grows by at most one bit per five data bits, never more.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Worst case for bit stuffing is an all-ones stream, and the count is &lfloor;n/5&rfloor;.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q40", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "framing-bit-byte-stuffing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The bit stream 0111110011111001 is received inside a bit-oriented frame, where the sender inserted a 0 after every five consecutive 1s. After de-stuffing, what is the original data?", "options": ["01111101111101", "01111110111101", "0111110111110", "01111001111101"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The receiver scans left to right, counts consecutive 1s, and deletes the 0 that immediately follows the fifth 1; the counter then restarts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Read <code>0</code>: no run yet. Then <code>11111</code> completes a run of five 1s.</li><li>The next bit is <code>0</code> and is the stuffed bit, so it is deleted; the counter restarts at 0.</li><li>The next bit is <code>0</code>, which is genuine data; the counter stays at 0.</li><li>Then <code>11111</code> completes another run of five, and the following <code>0</code> is deleted.</li><li>The last two bits <code>0</code> and <code>1</code> are genuine, giving <code>01111101111101</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; <code>01111101111101</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Deleting every 0 that follows a run of 1s, including the genuine data 0. Exactly one 0 is removed per completed run of five 1s, and the run counter resets after each removal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> De-stuffing is deterministic: one deletion per run of five 1s, then restart the count.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q41", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "framing-bit-byte-stuffing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about framing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Byte stuffing works on arbitrary binary data only if the escape byte itself is escaped.", "Bit stuffing changes the length of a frame by at most one bit.", "In HDLC a receiver that sees seven consecutive 1s treats the frame as aborted.", "Bit stuffing guarantees that the flag pattern never occurs inside the frame body."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Stuffing exists to keep the flag pattern out of the data. In byte-oriented framing the escape byte is data too, so it must escape itself; in bit-oriented framing the flag contains six consecutive 1s, which stuffing makes unreachable inside the body.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If the escape byte were not itself escaped, a data escape byte would be read as the start of an escape sequence: (A) is true.</li><li>A long frame of all 1s gains one bit per five, so the growth is proportional to the frame, not bounded by one bit: (B) is false.</li><li>HDLC reserves seven consecutive 1s as the abort sequence, which stuffing can never produce in valid data: (C) is true.</li><li>Stuffing removes any run of six 1s, and the flag <code>01111110</code> contains six, so it cannot appear in the body: (D) is true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) confuses &lsquo;one bit per insertion&rsquo; with &lsquo;one bit per frame&rsquo;. The number of insertions scales with the length of the data.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Byte stuffing must escape the flag and the escape; bit stuffing must break any run of six 1s.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q42", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "framing-bit-byte-stuffing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about the robustness and overhead of the three framing methods are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In the worst case, byte stuffing can more than double the length of the frame body.", "A single corrupted count field in character-count framing destroys the framing of every following frame in the stream.", "Flag-based framing is self-synchronising: after an error the receiver can recover at the next flag.", "The overhead of bit stuffing is at most one extra bit for every five data bits."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The three methods trade overhead against recoverability. A count field is compact but has no redundancy, so one error desynchronises the stream; flags cost more but let the receiver resynchronise.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Byte stuffing inserts exactly one escape byte before each flag or escape byte, so the body at most doubles: (A) is false.</li><li>The count field is the only thing that tells the receiver where the frame ends; if it is wrong, every later frame boundary is wrong too: (B) is true.</li><li>A receiver that loses its place scans for the next flag pattern and resumes there: (C) is true.</li><li>Stuffing inserts one bit per completed run of five 1s, so overhead never exceeds one bit in six: (D) is true.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (A) feels plausible because escaping every byte doubles the size, but each escaped byte becomes exactly two bytes &mdash; never three &mdash; so doubling is the exact worst case, not an underestimate of it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Character count is cheapest and most fragile; flags cost more and recover themselves.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q43", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "framing-bit-byte-stuffing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A frame carries 300 bytes of data, of which 12 bytes are 0x7E and 5 bytes are 0x7D. Byte stuffing inserts ESC = 0x7D before each such byte. How many bytes are transmitted between the opening and closing flags?", "options": ["300", "305", "312", "317"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Byte stuffing leaves ordinary bytes alone and turns each flag or escape byte into a two-byte sequence, so the body grows by exactly the number of such bytes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The 12 bytes equal to 0x7E each become two bytes: 12 extra bytes.</li><li>The 5 bytes equal to 0x7D are escaped as well, since the escape byte must escape itself: 5 extra bytes.</li><li>The other 300 &minus; 12 &minus; 5 = 283 bytes are transmitted unchanged.</li><li>Total between the flags = 283 + 12 &times; 2 + 5 &times; 2 = 283 + 24 + 10 = 317 bytes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 317 bytes &mdash; option (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that 0x7D is the escape byte and so must be escaped itself, which gives 312. Counting the opening and closing flags inside the figure is the other slip; they are asked for separately.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Escaped bytes double, and the escape byte is one of them.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q44", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following schemes can correct a single-bit error?", "options": ["A single parity bit per block", "A block code with minimum Hamming distance 2", "A Hamming code with minimum distance 3", "A 16-bit Internet checksum"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Correction needs the received word to be nearer one codeword than any other, which requires minimum distance at least 3; distance 2 only guarantees detection.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A code with minimum distance <code>d</code> corrects <code>&lfloor;(d &minus; 1)/2&rfloor;</code> errors.</li><li>A single parity bit gives <code>d = 2</code>, so it corrects 0 errors.</li><li>A code with <code>d = 2</code> also corrects 0 errors &mdash; it detects single errors only.</li><li>A checksum is an arithmetic check with no distance guarantee, so it cannot locate an error at all.</li><li>A Hamming code with <code>d = 3</code> corrects <code>&lfloor;2/2&rfloor; = 1</code> error.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; a Hamming code with minimum distance 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting a parity bit to point at the bad bit. Parity tells you that <i>something</i> is wrong; locating it needs several overlapping parity groups, which is exactly what a Hamming code builds.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Detect needs <code>d &ge; e + 1</code>; correct needs <code>d &ge; 2t + 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q45", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A single parity bit is appended to a block of data. Which error pattern is guaranteed to be detected?", "options": ["Any single-bit error", "Any two-bit error", "Any odd number of bit errors", "Any burst of three consecutive bit errors"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A single parity bit records the parity of the block, so it detects exactly those error patterns that change the parity, that is, patterns of odd weight.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The parity bit is chosen so that the total number of 1s has a fixed parity.</li><li>Flipping one bit changes that parity, so any single-bit error is detected &mdash; but that is a special case, not the general guarantee.</li><li>Flipping two bits leaves the parity unchanged, so a two-bit error is undetected.</li><li>Flipping any odd number of bits changes the parity, so every odd-weight pattern is detected.</li><li>A burst of three flips the parity too, but a burst of two does not, so the burst statement is not a guarantee.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; any odd number of bit errors.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing parity detects all single-bit errors and therefore all small errors. Parity is blind to every even-weight pattern, including the very common two-bit error.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Single parity detects odd weight, nothing else.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q46", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A CRC uses the generator polynomial x3 + x + 1, whose bit pattern is 1011. The message bits are 1100 and three zeros are appended before division. Enter the decimal value of the resulting 3-bit remainder.", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CRC is polynomial division over GF(2): subtraction is XOR, the message is shifted left by the degree of the generator, and the remainder is the check bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Degree of the generator is 3, so append three zeros: <code>1100000</code>.</li><li>XOR <code>1100000</code> with <code>1011</code> aligned at the left: <code>1100 &oplus; 1011 = 0111</code>, giving <code>0111000</code>.</li><li>Bring the leading 1 into position and XOR again: <code>1110 &oplus; 1011 = 0101</code>, leaving <code>010100</code>.</li><li>Once more: <code>1010 &oplus; 1011 = 0001</code>, leaving <code>00010</code>.</li><li>No further leading 1 remains, so the 3-bit remainder is <code>010</code> = 2 in decimal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 (remainder <code>010</code>).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Subtracting in ordinary integer arithmetic. Over GF(2) there is no borrow: <code>1100 &oplus; 1011 = 0111</code>, not <code>0001</code>. The other slip is appending two zeros instead of three, which silently changes the remainder.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Append as many zeros as the generator's degree, then XOR-shift; never subtract.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q47", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A CRC generator polynomial has degree r. Which class of burst errors is it guaranteed to detect?", "options": ["Every burst error of length at most r", "Every burst error of length at most r + 1", "Every burst error of length exactly r + 1", "Every burst error, whatever its length"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A degree-<code>r</code> generator has a non-zero constant term, so any error polynomial of degree below <code>r</code> cannot be a multiple of it; that is the burst guarantee.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A burst of length <code>k</code> is an error polynomial of the form <code>x<sup>i</sup>(1 + &hellip; + x<sup>k&minus;1</sup>)</code>.</li><li>The generator <code>G(x)</code> of degree <code>r</code> divides such a polynomial only if it divides the bracket, which has degree <code>k &minus; 1</code>.</li><li>When <code>k &le; r</code> the bracket has degree below <code>r</code>, so it cannot be a non-zero multiple of <code>G(x)</code> and the error is always detected.</li><li>Bursts longer than <code>r</code> can slip through, so the guarantee stops exactly at <code>r</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; every burst of length at most <code>r</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Extending the guarantee to <code>r + 1</code> or claiming CRC detects everything. A CRC detects all single-bit errors, all odd numbers of errors when <code>G</code> has <code>(x + 1)</code> as a factor, and all bursts up to length <code>r</code> &mdash; and nothing more in general.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Degree <code>r</code> buys burst protection up to length <code>r</code>, full stop.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q48", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about error-detecting and error-correcting codes are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The Internet checksum adds the 16-bit words using two's complement arithmetic.", "A code with minimum Hamming distance d detects any pattern of at most d − 1 bit errors.", "A code with minimum Hamming distance d corrects any pattern of at most ⌊(d − 1)/2⌋ bit errors.", "Adding one parity bit to a block raises the minimum distance of the code from 1 to 2."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Minimum distance fixes both guarantees: detection up to <code>d &minus; 1</code>, correction up to <code>&lfloor;(d &minus; 1)/2&rfloor;</code>. The Internet checksum is one's complement, not two's complement.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Two codewords at distance <code>d</code> cannot be joined by an error of weight below <code>d</code>, so any such error lands off the code: (B) is true.</li><li>Correcting <code>t</code> errors needs disjoint balls of radius <code>t</code>, which requires <code>d &ge; 2t + 1</code>, hence <code>t = &lfloor;(d &minus; 1)/2&rfloor;</code>: (C) is true.</li><li>Without a parity bit any word is a codeword, so <code>d = 1</code>; one parity bit makes every pair differ in at least two places: (D) is true.</li><li>The Internet checksum uses one's complement arithmetic with end-around carry: (A) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (A) is a favourite trap because two's complement is the arithmetic most programmers reach for. The Internet checksum predates that habit: it is one's complement, which is why the end-around carry exists and why there are two representations of zero.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Distance drives the guarantees; the Internet checksum is one's complement with end-around carry.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q49", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A block code has minimum Hamming distance 6. Which of the following states the largest guaranteed detection and correction capability?", "options": ["Detect up to 5 bit errors, correct up to 2", "Detect up to 5 bit errors, correct up to 3", "Detect up to 6 bit errors, correct up to 2", "Detect up to 6 bit errors, correct up to 3"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Detection is guaranteed up to <code>d &minus; 1</code> and correction up to <code>&lfloor;(d &minus; 1)/2&rfloor;</code>; with <code>d = 6</code> these are 5 and 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>d &minus; 1 = 5</code>, so every pattern of weight at most 5 is detected.</li><li><code>&lfloor;(6 &minus; 1)/2&rfloor; = &lfloor;2.5&rfloor; = 2</code>, so up to 2 errors can be corrected.</li><li>Correcting 3 errors would need <code>d &ge; 2 &times; 3 + 1 = 7</code>, which this code does not have.</li><li>Detecting 6 errors would need <code>d &ge; 7</code>, and a weight-6 error can land exactly on another codeword at distance 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; detect 5, correct 2.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rounding <code>(d &minus; 1)/2</code> up, or assuming detection and correction capacities can both be pushed to the maximum at once. The two guarantees are separate budgets taken from the same distance.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With <code>d = 6</code>: detect 5, correct 2 &mdash; the floor, never the ceiling.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U1-Q50", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "network-fundamentals-framing", "subtopic": "error-detection-crc-checksum-hamming", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A single-error-correcting Hamming code uses r = 6 parity bits. What is the largest number of data bits it can protect?", "options": [], "answer_display": "57", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With <code>r</code> parity bits the syndrome has <code>2<sup>r</sup></code> values: one for &lsquo;no error&rsquo; and one for each of the <code>m + r</code> single-bit positions. Hence <code>m + r + 1 &le; 2<sup>r</sup></code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Write the Hamming condition: <code>m + r + 1 &le; 2<sup>r</sup></code>.</li><li>Substitute <code>r = 6</code>: <code>2<sup>6</sup> = 64</code>, so <code>m + 6 + 1 &le; 64</code>.</li><li>Therefore <code>m &le; 64 &minus; 7 = 57</code>.</li><li>With <code>m = 57</code> the code has 57 + 6 = 63 bit positions, and the 64th syndrome value is reserved for &lsquo;no error&rsquo;.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 57 data bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code>2<sup>r</sup> &ge; m + r</code> and answering 58, which forgets the all-zero syndrome that must mean &lsquo;no error&rsquo;. The <code>+1</code> in the inequality is that reserved pattern.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A single-error-correcting Hamming code spends one syndrome value on &lsquo;no error&rsquo;: <code>m &le; 2<sup>r</sup> &minus; r &minus; 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q01", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "stop-and-wait-arq", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A sender uses stop-and-wait ARQ over a 1 Mbps link whose one-way propagation delay is 20 ms. Frames are 1000 bits long and acknowledgements are negligible. What is the link utilisation?", "options": ["2.44%", "4.88%", "50%", "97.56%"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Stop-and-wait utilisation is the fraction of the cycle during which the sender is actually transmitting. One cycle lasts one frame transmission plus one round trip.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Transmission time of a frame: <code>Tt = L/R = 1000/10⁶ = 1 ms</code>.</li><li>Round-trip propagation delay: <code>2 × 20 = 40 ms</code>.</li><li>Cycle length: <code>1 + 40 = 41 ms</code>.</li><li>Utilisation: <code>U = 1/41 ≈ 0.02439</code>, that is <code>2.44%</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 2.44%.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using only the one-way delay gives <code>1/21 ≈ 4.76%</code>, which is close to the 4.88% distractor. An ACK must travel back, so the wait is always <code>2 × Tp</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code>U = Tt/(Tt + 2Tp) = 1/(1 + 2a)</code> with <code>a = Tp/Tt</code>. Compute <code>a</code> first; if <code>a</code> is large the protocol is hopeless however fast the link.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q02", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "stop-and-wait-arq", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a stop-and-wait ARQ connection the acknowledgement for a correctly received frame is lost. What does the receiver do when the sender's retransmission of that frame arrives, and why is a 1-bit sequence number enough?", "options": ["It discards the frame as a duplicate and repeats the acknowledgement; one bit suffices because at most one frame is outstanding, so the receiver only has to tell a new frame from a copy of the previous one.", "It accepts the frame as new data; one bit prevents this by making the sender use a different number each time.", "It discards the frame; one bit suffices because a stop-and-wait sender never retransmits.", "It discards the frame; two bits are needed because the receiver must distinguish four protocol states."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Stop-and-wait keeps at most one unacknowledged frame in flight, so the sequence number only has to separate &ldquo;the frame I am expecting&rdquo; from &ldquo;the frame I already have&rdquo;. Alternating 0 and 1 does exactly that.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The sender transmits frame 0; the receiver accepts it and replies with an acknowledgement that means &ldquo;I have 0, send 1&rdquo;.</li><li>That acknowledgement is lost, so the sender's timer expires and it retransmits frame 0.</li><li>The receiver is expecting 1 and sees 0, so it recognises a duplicate, discards the payload and re-sends the same acknowledgement.</li><li>Because the sender cannot have two frames outstanding, the receiver never needs to name more than two possibilities, and one bit covers them.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Candidates often think the lost acknowledgement corrupts data or that more sequence bits are needed. The payload was never damaged; only the confirmation was, and a duplicate must be recognised and acknowledged again.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The sequence-number space of a protocol is set by the largest number of frames that can be outstanding, not by the link speed. One outstanding frame &rArr; 2 numbers &rArr; 1 bit.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q03", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "stop-and-wait-arq", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Frames are <code>L</code> bits, acknowledgements are <code>A</code> bits, the link bandwidth is <code>R</code> bits/s and the one-way propagation delay is <code>d</code> seconds. Ignoring processing and queueing, the stop-and-wait utilisation is:", "options": ["L/(L + A + 2dR)", "L/(L + A + dR)", "L/(2L + A + 2dR)", "L/(L + 2A + 2dR)"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Utilisation is useful transmission time divided by the length of one complete cycle. In stop-and-wait a cycle contains the frame transmission, the acknowledgement transmission and one round trip of propagation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Frame transmission: <code>L/R</code>.</li><li>Acknowledgement transmission: <code>A/R</code>.</li><li>Propagation there and back: <code>2d</code>.</li><li>Cycle: <code>L/R + A/R + 2d = (L + A + 2dR)/R</code>.</li><li>Utilisation: <code>(L/R) ÷ ((L + A + 2dR)/R) = L/(L + A + 2dR)</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) counts the propagation delay once, forgetting that the acknowledgement has to come back. Option (C) charges the frame twice, which would be a pipelining error, and (D) charges the acknowledgement twice.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Derive the cycle once and keep it: <code>Tt + Tack + 2Tp</code>. Every stop-and-wait efficiency question in GATE is this expression with numbers substituted.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q04", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "stop-and-wait-arq", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about stop-and-wait ARQ are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A 1-bit sequence number in each direction is sufficient to run the protocol correctly.", "Its efficiency does not depend on the propagation delay.", "If an acknowledgement is lost, the sender retransmits after a timeout and the receiver discards the duplicate.", "Its utilisation can never exceed 50%, even when the propagation delay is zero."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Stop-and-wait is a one-outstanding-frame protocol: its correctness comes from alternating sequence numbers and a timeout, and its efficiency comes from <code>Tt/(Tt + 2Tp)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. At most one frame is in flight, so 0/1 alternation names every case the receiver must distinguish.</li><li>(B) False. <code>U = Tt/(Tt + 2Tp)</code> falls as <code>Tp</code> grows; a satellite link with the same frame size and bandwidth has far lower utilisation than a short cable.</li><li>(C) True. The timeout is the only recovery mechanism for a lost acknowledgement; the receiver then sees a duplicate sequence number and discards it.</li><li>(D) False. As <code>Tp &rarr; 0</code>, <code>U &rarr; Tt/Tt = 1</code>, that is 100%, not 50%.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The 50% figure is confused with the efficiency of a protocol that waits for an acknowledgement <i>and</i> then idles for an equal time, or with the ALOHA throughput limit. Stop-and-wait has no such ceiling.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Judge a protocol by its cycle: stop-and-wait pays one round trip per frame, which is why long fat links force a pipelined protocol.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q05", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "stop-and-wait-arq", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A stop-and-wait sender transmits 1000-bit frames over a 1 Mbps link. The one-way propagation delay is 2 ms and the acknowledgement transmission time is negligible.<br/>What is the link utilisation, in percent?", "options": [], "answer_display": "20", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Utilisation is the fraction of the cycle spent transmitting the useful frame: <code>U = Tt/(Tt + 2Tp)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>Tt = 1000/10⁶ = 1 ms</code>.</li><li><code>2Tp = 2 × 2 = 4 ms</code>.</li><li>Cycle <code>= 1 + 4 = 5 ms</code>.</li><li><code>U = 1/5 = 0.2</code>, i.e. 20%.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 20 (percent).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing by <code>1 + Tp</code> gives <code>1/3 ≈ 33.3%</code>; the acknowledgement's return trip must be counted as well.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Note how little of the link is used: a five-fold speed-up of the link would not change this figure at all, because the cycle is dominated by propagation.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q06", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "go-back-n-selective-repeat-efficiency", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A Go-Back-N sender uses a 3-bit sequence number field. What is the largest window size it can use?", "options": ["4", "7", "8", "15"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With <code>m</code> sequence bits there are <code>2ᵐ</code> distinct numbers. Go-Back-N must leave one number unused so that the receiver can always separate a new frame from a retransmission.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>2ᵐ = 2³ = 8</code> sequence numbers, 0 to 7.</li><li>Go-Back-N allows a window of at most <code>2ᵐ − 1 = 7</code>.</li><li>If the window were 8, the sender could have frames 0&ndash;7 outstanding; after 7 is acknowledged and 0 is sent again, an old duplicate 0 could be accepted as new.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 7.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing 8 (the whole space) is the standard trap; 4 is the selective-repeat bound <code>2ᵐ⁻¹</code>, and 15 belongs to a 4-bit field.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Remember the pair: Go-Back-N <code>2ᵐ − 1</code>, selective repeat <code>2ᵐ⁻¹</code>. Both are one less than the naive guess.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q07", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "go-back-n-selective-repeat-efficiency", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A Go-Back-N sender with a window of 4 has sent frames 1, 2, 3 and 4. Frame 2 is lost, but frames 3 and 4 arrive correctly. What happens next?", "options": ["The receiver buffers 3 and 4 and acknowledges each of them separately, so only frame 2 is retransmitted.", "The receiver discards 3 and 4 and repeats the acknowledgement of frame 1; the sender then retransmits 2, 3 and 4.", "The receiver buffers 3 and 4 and sends a cumulative acknowledgement for frame 4.", "The receiver sends a negative acknowledgement for frame 2, and the sender retransmits only frame 2."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A Go-Back-N receiver accepts frames strictly in order and acknowledges cumulatively. Anything that arrives out of order is discarded, and the sender's window slides back to the first unacknowledged frame.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The receiver is waiting for frame 2 and holds a cumulative acknowledgement covering frame 1.</li><li>Frames 3 and 4 arrive out of order, so the receiver discards them and re-sends the acknowledgement for frame 1.</li><li>The sender's timer for frame 2 expires; its window still starts at 2.</li><li>The sender retransmits 2, 3 and 4 &mdash; it &ldquo;goes back N&rdquo; &mdash; and the receiver accepts them in order.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Options (A) and (D) describe selective repeat, which buffers out-of-order frames and retransmits only the missing one. Go-Back-N does neither.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The price of Go-Back-N's simple receiver is wasted bandwidth on every error: the whole window after the lost frame is sent again.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q08", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "go-back-n-selective-repeat-efficiency", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "With <code>m</code>-bit sequence numbers a Go-Back-N sender may use a window of <code>2ᵐ − 1</code>, but a selective-repeat sender may use only <code>2ᵐ⁻¹</code>. Why?", "options": ["Because the selective-repeat receiver accepts out-of-order frames, so a window wider than half the sequence space would let a retransmitted frame from the previous window be mistaken for a new frame.", "Because selective repeat needs a larger send buffer, and the buffer is limited by the sequence-number space.", "Because Go-Back-N uses cumulative acknowledgements and selective repeat does not use acknowledgements at all.", "Because selective repeat numbers frames from 1 while Go-Back-N numbers them from 0, which halves the usable range."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A selective-repeat receiver can hold frames from several windows at once. Its window must be at most half the sequence space so that the sets &ldquo;frames the receiver may still accept&rdquo; and &ldquo;frames the sender may still retransmit&rdquo; never overlap.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let the window be <code>W</code> and the space be <code>2ᵐ</code>. Suppose <code>W &gt; 2ᵐ⁻¹</code>.</li><li>After all outstanding frames are acknowledged, the sender's window advances by <code>W</code>, which is more than half the space.</li><li>The receiver's window has also advanced, but frames of the previous window may still be in flight or buffered.</li><li>The two windows then overlap on some sequence number, so an old duplicate and a new frame carry the same number and the receiver cannot tell them apart.</li><li>Keeping <code>W &le; 2ᵐ⁻¹</code> makes the windows disjoint and the ambiguity disappears; Go-Back-N needs no such restriction because its receiver never buffers out-of-order frames, so only the single unacknowledged frame can be ambiguous.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> The bound is often memorised as a fact about buffering or acknowledgement style. It is a window-overlap argument: the receiver must never confuse a retransmission with a fresh frame.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Derive window bounds from &ldquo;do the sender's and receiver's windows overlap?&rdquo; rather than memorising <code>2ᵐ − 1</code> and <code>2ᵐ⁻¹</code> as unrelated facts.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q09", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "go-back-n-selective-repeat-efficiency", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements comparing Go-Back-N with selective repeat are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Go-Back-N acknowledges cumulatively, while selective repeat acknowledges frames individually.", "A standard Go-Back-N receiver must buffer a full window of out-of-order frames.", "Selective repeat retransmits only the frames that were actually lost, whereas Go-Back-N retransmits the lost frame and every frame sent after it.", "With <code>m</code>-bit sequence numbers a Go-Back-N window may be at most <code>2ᵐ − 1</code>."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two protocols trade receiver complexity against bandwidth. Go-Back-N has a trivial receiver and wastes bandwidth after an error; selective repeat has a buffering receiver and wastes nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. One cumulative acknowledgement covers a whole run of frames; selective repeat needs a per-frame acknowledgement.</li><li>(B) False. A standard Go-Back-N receiver keeps only the expected sequence number and discards everything else, so its buffer holds one frame.</li><li>(C) True. That is exactly the trade-off: selective repeat resends the missing frame alone.</li><li>(D) True. One number must be left unused so that a duplicate cannot be accepted as new.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Many candidates assume the protocol with the larger window must also buffer more; here the buffering is in the receiver and the window bound has a different cause.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Answer comparison questions by listing, for each protocol, what the sender stores, what the receiver stores, and what is resent after a loss.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q10", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "go-back-n-selective-repeat-efficiency", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A selective-repeat sender uses a 4-bit sequence number field.<br/>What is the maximum number of frames it may have outstanding at any time?", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A selective-repeat window may be at most half the sequence space, so that the sender's and receiver's windows never overlap.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>With 4 bits there are <code>2⁴ = 16</code> sequence numbers.</li><li>Maximum window <code>= 2ᵐ⁻¹ = 2³ = 8</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8 frames.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 16 uses the whole space; with 16 outstanding frames a retransmission of frame 0 would be indistinguishable from a new frame 0.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a given <code>m</code>: Go-Back-N gives <code>2ᵐ − 1</code>, selective repeat gives <code>2ᵐ⁻¹</code>. Halving, then rounding down, is the quick mental check for selective repeat.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q11", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "pure-and-slotted-aloha", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "What is the maximum achievable throughput of pure ALOHA, as a fraction of the channel capacity?", "options": ["18.4%", "36.8%", "50%", "100%"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In pure ALOHA a frame is destroyed if any other frame starts within one frame time before or after its own start, so the vulnerable period is two frame times. With Poisson arrivals the throughput is <code>S = G·e⁻²ᴳ</code>, where <code>G</code> is the offered load in frames per frame time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Differentiate: <code>dS/dG = e⁻²ᴳ(1 − 2G)</code>, which is zero at <code>G = 0.5</code>.</li><li>Substitute: <code>S = 0.5·e⁻¹ = 1/(2e)</code>.</li><li><code>1/(2e) ≈ 0.184</code>, that is 18.4%.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 18.4%.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 36.8% is the slotted-ALOHA figure <code>1/e</code>. Slotted ALOHA halves the vulnerable period to one frame time, which doubles the maximum throughput.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Keep the pair together: pure <code>1/(2e) ≈ 0.184</code> at <code>G = 0.5</code>; slotted <code>1/e ≈ 0.368</code> at <code>G = 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q12", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "pure-and-slotted-aloha", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Ten stations share a slotted ALOHA channel and each transmits in a given slot with probability 0.1, independently of the others. What is the probability that a given slot is successful?", "options": ["0.100", "0.349", "0.387", "0.651"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A slot succeeds when exactly one of the <code>N</code> stations transmits and the other <code>N − 1</code> stay silent: <code>P = N·p·(1 − p)ᴺ⁻¹</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>N = 10</code>, <code>p = 0.1</code>.</li><li><code>P = 10 × 0.1 × 0.9⁹ = 0.9⁹</code>.</li><li><code>0.9⁹ = 0.38742…</code>, so about 0.387.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 0.387.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dropping the factor 10 gives <code>0.1 × 0.9⁹ ≈ 0.0387</code>, which is the probability that one <i>named</i> station transmits and nobody else does. The 0.651 distractor is <code>1 − 0.9¹⁰</code>, the probability that at least one station transmits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Distinguish three events: a named station succeeding alone, some station succeeding alone, and at least one transmitting. Only the middle one is ALOHA throughput.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q13", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "pure-and-slotted-aloha", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about ALOHA are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The vulnerable period of pure ALOHA is two frame times, while that of slotted ALOHA is one frame time.", "Slotted ALOHA requires all stations to be synchronised to common slot boundaries.", "Slotted ALOHA doubles the maximum throughput of pure ALOHA.", "In pure ALOHA a station that suffers a collision retransmits immediately, in the next slot."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The vulnerable period is the interval during which another station's start would destroy the frame. Synchronising starts to slot boundaries removes the &ldquo;half a frame before&rdquo; risk, halving the vulnerable period and doubling the throughput.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Pure ALOHA: any start in the preceding or the following frame time collides, so two frame times. Slotted: only a start in the same slot collides, so one.</li><li>(B) True. Without a common clock there are no slot boundaries, so the protocol degenerates to pure ALOHA.</li><li>(C) True. <code>1/e</code> against <code>1/(2e)</code>: exactly twice.</li><li>(D) False. Immediate retransmission guarantees a repeat collision; a random backoff is required, which is why ALOHA is a random-access protocol.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that slotted ALOHA removes collisions. It only removes collisions caused by starts in the previous frame time; two stations in the same slot still collide.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Every throughput improvement in this family comes from shrinking the vulnerable period: pure 2T, slotted T, CSMA far less.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q14", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "pure-and-slotted-aloha", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a pure ALOHA network the frames are 1000 bits long and the channel rate is 1 Mbps.<br/>What is the vulnerable period of a frame, in milliseconds?", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The vulnerable period of pure ALOHA is the window in which any other transmission start destroys the frame: one frame time before its start plus one frame time after it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Frame time <code>Tfr = L/R = 1000/10⁶ = 10⁻³ s = 1 ms</code>.</li><li>Vulnerable period <code>= 2·Tfr = 2 ms</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 ms.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 1 ms gives the frame time, or the vulnerable period of <i>slotted</i> ALOHA. In pure ALOHA a station that starts half a frame before the frame of interest still overlaps it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The factor 2 in <code>e⁻²ᴳ</code> is exactly this doubling of the vulnerable period; the exponent is not a constant to memorise.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q15", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "pure-and-slotted-aloha", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A slotted ALOHA system in which stations retransmit with a fixed probability after a collision is said to be unstable. Which statement explains why?", "options": ["Throughput follows <code>S = G·e⁻ᴳ</code>; when the backlog of collided stations grows, the offered load <code>G</code> rises past the peak, throughput falls, and the backlog then grows without bound.", "The system is stable for every arrival rate below <code>1/e ≈ 0.368</code>, so instability can never occur.", "Throughput is maximised when every station transmits in every slot, so stability requires retransmission probability 1.", "Slots are synchronised, so collisions cannot occur and the system is stable by construction."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Slotted ALOHA throughput is <code>S(G) = G·e⁻ᴳ</code>, which rises to a peak at <code>G = 1</code> and then falls. Stability is about the feedback between backlog and offered load, not about the peak value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Offered load = new arrivals plus retransmissions of backlogged stations.</li><li>If the backlog rises while the retransmission probability is fixed, retransmissions rise in proportion, so <code>G</code> increases.</li><li>Once <code>G &gt; 1</code> the curve is decreasing: more offered load yields <i>less</i> throughput.</li><li>Fewer successes means a larger backlog next slot, so <code>G</code> rises again: positive feedback, and the channel collapses towards <code>S &rarr; 0</code>.</li><li>A stable design makes the retransmission probability shrink as the estimated backlog grows, so the system settles near the peak.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) confuses the peak throughput <code>1/e</code> with the stability limit. For Poisson arrivals the slotted-ALOHA channel is unstable for every arrival rate above zero without backlog control.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask whether the operating point is on the rising or the falling side of <code>S(G)</code>. A protocol that adds load in response to failure operates on the falling side.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q16", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "csma-cd-csma-ca-backoff", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Why does CSMA/CD impose a minimum frame size?", "options": ["So that the sender is still transmitting when a collision signal can reach it, i.e. the frame time must be at least twice the maximum one-way propagation delay.", "So that the receiver can buffer the whole frame before checking the CRC.", "So that the frame is long enough for the preamble to be detected reliably.", "So that every station obtains an equal share of the channel."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CSMA/CD detects a collision by listening while transmitting. That works only if the sender has not finished before the corrupted signal comes back, which takes at most one round trip across the collision domain.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Worst case: station A starts transmitting and station B starts just before A's signal arrives.</li><li>B's collision signal needs almost one full one-way delay to reach A, and A's own signal needed one to reach B.</li><li>So A must still be transmitting after <code>2τ</code>, where <code>τ</code> is the maximum one-way delay.</li><li>Hence <code>Tt ≥ 2τ</code>, i.e. <code>L/R ≥ 2τ</code>: a minimum frame size.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the bound is <code>τ</code> rather than <code>2τ</code>, which halves the minimum frame and lets late collisions go undetected.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code>2τ</code> is the slot time. Every Ethernet standard sets the minimum frame from its slot time, which is why the minimum grew from 64 bytes at 10 Mbps to 512 bytes at Gigabit speed over the same cable.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q17", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "csma-cd-csma-ca-backoff", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A CSMA/CD LAN runs at 100 Mbps over a 1 km cable on which signals travel at 2 × 10⁸ m/s. What is the minimum frame size?", "options": ["62.5 bytes", "125 bytes", "250 bytes", "1000 bytes"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The minimum frame must last at least one round trip across the collision domain: <code>L ≥ R × 2τ</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>τ = 1000 m ÷ (2 × 10⁸ m/s) = 5 × 10⁻⁶ s = 5 μs</code>.</li><li><code>2τ = 10 μs</code>.</li><li><code>L = 100 Mbps × 10 μs = 10⁸ × 10⁻⁵ = 1000 bits</code>.</li><li><code>1000 bits ÷ 8 = 125 bytes</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 125 bytes.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code>τ</code> alone gives 500 bits, that is 62.5 bytes, which is offered as a distractor. 250 bytes doubles the wrong quantity instead.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Work in seconds first, then convert to bytes only at the end; mixing bits and bytes mid-calculation is where most marks are lost.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q18", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "csma-cd-csma-ca-backoff", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "An 802.11 station running CSMA/CA waits a random backoff even when it has found the channel idle for a DIFS, whereas an Ethernet station running CSMA/CD backs off only after a collision has been detected. Why the difference?", "options": ["Because a wireless station cannot reliably detect collisions while transmitting &mdash; its own transmission swamps the weak received signal, and hidden terminals collide without either station hearing the other &mdash; so collisions must be avoided in advance instead of detected afterwards.", "Because wireless links are faster, so a collision wastes less time and detection is unnecessary.", "Because 802.11 has no acknowledgement mechanism, so a station can never learn that its frame was lost.", "Because 802.11 stations do not perform carrier sensing at all and therefore cannot use collision detection."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Collision <i>detection</i> needs a station to hear a second signal while it transmits. Collision <i>avoidance</i> replaces that with a randomised waiting time before transmitting, plus an explicit acknowledgement afterwards.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>On a wire, a transmitting station can compare what it sends with what it receives, and the difference reveals a collision.</li><li>On a radio, the transmitted power is enormously larger than any received signal at the same antenna, so the comparison is impossible.</li><li>A hidden terminal is out of range of the sender but in range of the receiver; the two collide and neither transmitter can tell.</li><li>802.11 therefore waits a random number of slots after the channel becomes idle, which separates contenders before they start.</li><li>Because collisions cannot be detected, the receiver must acknowledge each frame, and the sender's failure to hear the acknowledgement is what triggers exponential backoff.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (D) is a popular error: 802.11 does sense the carrier (physically and virtually, through the network allocation vector). It senses but cannot <i>detect collisions</i>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Detection requires duplex hearing; a medium that cannot provide it forces avoidance plus acknowledgement.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q19", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "csma-cd-csma-ca-backoff", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about carrier-sense protocols are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A 1-persistent CSMA station transmits as soon as it finds the channel idle.", "A non-persistent CSMA station that finds the channel busy waits a random time before sensing again, which reduces collisions at the cost of longer delays.", "CSMA/CD detects a collision by comparing the signal it transmits with the signal it receives.", "A p-persistent CSMA station transmits with probability <code>p</code> in each slot while the channel is busy."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The persistence rule decides what a station does when the channel is busy or becomes idle, and that rule is the whole difference between the three variants.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. That eagerness is what the name means, and it is why several waiting stations collide as soon as the channel frees.</li><li>(B) True. Backing off when busy spreads the retries out; the price is idle time that a persistent station would have used.</li><li>(C) True. The difference between the sent and received waveforms is the collision signature.</li><li>(D) False. p-persistent CSMA transmits with probability <code>p</code> in each slot once the channel is found <i>idle</i>; while the channel is busy it simply waits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reversing the condition in (D). Persistence is about how aggressively a station seizes an idle channel, not about transmitting into a busy one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Rank the variants by aggressiveness: 1-persistent (most collisions, least delay), p-persistent, non-persistent (fewest collisions, most delay).</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q20", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "csma-cd-csma-ca-backoff", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A 10 Mbps CSMA/CD LAN has a slot time, that is a maximum round-trip propagation delay, of 51.2 μs.<br/>What is the minimum frame size, in bits?", "options": [], "answer_display": "512", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The frame must last at least one slot time so that a collision is still heard while the sender is transmitting: <code>L = R × slot time</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>R = 10 Mbps = 10⁷ bits/s</code>.</li><li>Slot time <code>= 51.2 μs = 51.2 × 10⁻⁶ s</code>.</li><li><code>L = 10⁷ × 51.2 × 10⁻⁶ = 512 bits</code>.</li><li>In bytes, <code>512/8 = 64</code> bytes, the familiar Ethernet minimum.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 512 bits (64 bytes).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Halving to 256 bits by using the one-way delay instead of the round trip, or answering 64 because the question asked for bits while 64 is the byte figure.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The 64-byte minimum of 10 Mbps Ethernet is not arbitrary: it is 512 bit times, the slot time of a 2500 m collision domain.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q21", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "ethernet-standards-switches-bridges", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which device learns source MAC addresses and then forwards a frame only to the port on which the destination address was last seen?", "options": ["A repeater", "A hub", "A transparent bridge (layer-2 switch)", "A router"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A transparent bridge builds a forwarding table by watching the source address of every frame it receives, and uses the destination address to choose one output port.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Repeaters and hubs are physical-layer devices: they regenerate or repeat every bit out of every other port, with no addresses at all.</li><li>A bridge reads the frame header, records <code>(source MAC, arrival port)</code> in its table, and looks up the destination.</li><li>If the destination is known, the frame leaves exactly one port; if not, it is flooded.</li><li>A router works on network-layer addresses and does not switch on MAC addresses.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; a transparent bridge (layer-2 switch).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing a hub with a switch because both have several ports. A hub is a shared wire; a switch is a set of independent links joined by a table.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Layer 1 repeats, layer 2 switches, layer 3 routes. Ask which address the device reads to decide which it is.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q22", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "ethernet-standards-switches-bridges", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A switch receives a frame whose destination MAC address is not present in its forwarding table. What does it do?", "options": ["It discards the frame and waits for the address to be learned.", "It floods the frame out of every port except the one on which it arrived.", "It forwards the frame to the port with the lowest port number.", "It sends the frame back out of the port on which it arrived."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An unknown unicast address is handled exactly like a broadcast: the switch has no information, so it takes the only safe action and copies the frame to all other ports.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The switch looks up the destination in its table and finds no entry.</li><li>It cannot know which segment holds the host, so it floods to all ports except the arrival port.</li><li>The destination host eventually replies, and that reply teaches the switch the correct port.</li><li>Later frames to that address are switched to a single port.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dropping the frame would make the network unusable until every address had been learned; sending it back out of the arrival port would be pointless.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Flooding is not an error path; it is how learning starts. The only traffic a switch never floods is a frame whose destination port equals its arrival port (which it filters).</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q23", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "ethernet-standards-switches-bridges", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about Ethernet and its interconnection devices are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A layer-2 switch divides a collision domain but not a broadcast domain.", "A router divides both the collision domain and the broadcast domain.", "A hub forwards a frame only to the port on which the destination address was learned.", "802.3 Ethernet uses 48-bit MAC addresses and a minimum frame size of 64 bytes."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A collision domain is the set of stations whose transmissions can collide; a broadcast domain is the set that receives each other's broadcasts. Each device boundary removes one or both.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Each switch port is its own collision domain, but a broadcast is still flooded to all ports.</li><li>(B) True. A router terminates both: it does not forward broadcasts and it regenerates the frame on each interface.</li><li>(C) False. A hub has no table and no addresses; it repeats every bit out of every other port.</li><li>(D) True. Both figures are part of the 802.3 standard.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a switch also splits broadcast domains, which would make VLANs unnecessary. Only a router, or a VLAN boundary inside a switch, does that.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count domains device by device: hub &mdash; nothing split; switch &mdash; collision split; router &mdash; both split.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q24", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "ethernet-standards-switches-bridges", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A switch has 16 ports, and exactly one host is connected to each port.<br/>How many collision domains exist in this network?", "options": [], "answer_display": "16", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A switch gives every port its own collision domain, because it stores and forwards frames instead of repeating bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each port is a separate link with exactly one host on it.</li><li>Nothing transmitted on one port can collide with anything on another, so each port is one collision domain.</li><li>16 ports &rArr; 16 collision domains.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 1, as if the switch were a hub. A 16-port hub would be a single collision domain shared by all 16 hosts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> With full-duplex links the collision domain is not merely separate but empty &mdash; which is why modern switched Ethernet needs no CSMA/CD.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-DL2-Q25", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "ethernet-standards-switches-bridges", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Two transparent bridges are cabled so that they form a loop, and the spanning-tree protocol is disabled. What happens to a frame whose destination address is unknown to both bridges?", "options": ["It is flooded round the loop indefinitely and multiplies, because a layer-2 frame carries no hop count or time-to-live that a bridge could use to discard it.", "It is dropped as soon as it returns to the bridge that first forwarded it, because bridges detect duplicates by sequence number.", "It travels once round the loop and is then discarded when its time-to-live expires.", "Only broadcast frames loop; a unicast frame with an unknown address is returned to its source."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bridging is transparent and stateless with respect to forwarding: a bridge floods unknown destinations every time it sees them. With a loop, each flooded copy is seen again and flooded again.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Bridge 1 receives the frame, does not know the destination, and floods it to bridge 2.</li><li>Bridge 2 also does not know the destination, so it floods the frame back to bridge 1 and to its own segments.</li><li>Nothing in an 802.3 frame ages: there is no TTL field, and bridges do not keep a record of frames they have already forwarded.</li><li>Each pass creates more copies, so the copies grow without bound and the loop saturates &mdash; a broadcast storm.</li><li>Spanning tree protocol exists precisely to break the loop by blocking one port, keeping the topology a tree.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Options (B) and (C) assume layer-2 frames behave like IP packets. TTL, hop limits and duplicate detection are layer-3 and above; Ethernet has none of them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A layer-2 network must be loop-free by construction, because the frame itself carries no defence against looping.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q26", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "stop-and-wait-arq", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In stop-and-wait ARQ the sender arms a timer when it transmits a frame. Which value should the timeout be set to?", "options": ["One frame transmission time, <code>Tt</code>.", "One frame transmission time plus one round-trip propagation delay, plus the acknowledgement transmission time and a safety margin.", "Twice the one-way propagation delay, <code>2Tp</code>.", "The bandwidth-delay product of the link."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The timer has to cover everything that happens between the start of a transmission and the arrival of its acknowledgement: the frame on the way out, the propagation of both directions, the acknowledgement on the way back, and then a margin for processing and queueing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The frame itself occupies the link for <code>Tt = L/R</code>.</li><li>Its last bit then needs one propagation delay <code>Tp</code> to reach the receiver, and the acknowledgement needs another <code>Tp</code> to come back, so the round trip contributes <code>2Tp</code>.</li><li>The acknowledgement occupies the link for <code>Tack</code>.</li><li>A timeout of exactly <code>Tt + Tack + 2Tp</code> would fire on the fastest possible successful exchange, so real timers add a margin. Setting it to <code>Tt</code> alone or to <code>2Tp</code> alone fires before the acknowledgement could possibly arrive.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; frame time plus one round trip plus the acknowledgement time and a margin.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Many candidates answer <code>2Tp</code> because the propagation delay feels like the slow part. A timeout that ignores the frame transmission time expires while the frame is still leaving the sender, which produces a spurious retransmission every cycle.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> A timeout is a worst-case estimate of the whole exchange, not of one component of it.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q27", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "stop-and-wait-arq", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A stop-and-wait link achieves a utilisation of 20% when the acknowledgement time is negligible. If the one-way propagation delay is halved and nothing else changes, what is the new utilisation?", "options": ["40%", "20%", "33.3%", "10%"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With a negligible acknowledgement, the stop-and-wait utilisation is <code>U = Tt / (Tt + 2Tp)</code>. The ratio of the two times, not their absolute values, fixes the utilisation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Set <code>Tt/(Tt + 2Tp) = 0.2</code>.</li><li>Cross-multiplying: <code>Tt = 0.2Tt + 0.4Tp</code>, so <code>0.8Tt = 0.4Tp</code> and <code>2Tp = 4Tt</code>.</li><li>Halving the one-way delay halves the round trip: the new round trip is <code>2Tt</code>.</li><li>New utilisation <code>= Tt/(Tt + 2Tt) = 1/3 &asymp; 33.3%</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 33.3%.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Doubling the utilisation to 40% is the tempting error: halving the idle time does not double the fraction of time that is busy. Utilisation is a ratio <code>busy/(busy + idle)</code>, so it saturates towards 1 rather than scaling linearly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a fixed frame time, <code>U = 1/(1 + 2Tp/Tt)</code>: only the ratio <code>Tp/Tt</code> matters.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q28", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "stop-and-wait-arq", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A stop-and-wait sender uses 1000-byte frames on a 100 Mbps link whose one-way propagation delay is 1 ms; acknowledgements are negligible. What utilisation does it achieve?", "options": ["7.69%", "1.92%", "50%", "3.85%"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Utilisation is the fraction of the cycle spent transmitting: <code>U = Tt/(Tt + 2Tp)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>Tt = 8 &times; 1000 / 10<sup>8</sup> = 80 &micro;s</code>.</li><li><code>2Tp = 2 &times; 1 ms = 2000 &micro;s</code>.</li><li>Cycle <code>= 80 + 2000 = 2080 &micro;s</code>.</li><li><code>U = 80/2080 = 0.03846</code>, that is <code>3.85%</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; 3.85%.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 7.69% comes from using one propagation delay instead of the round trip; reporting 1.92% comes from counting four propagation delays. The acknowledgement must travel back, so exactly two one-way delays belong in the cycle.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> On a fast, long link the frame time vanishes beside the round trip, and stop-and-wait collapses to a few percent.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q29", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "stop-and-wait-arq", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about the receiver and the timer in stop-and-wait ARQ are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The receiver needs to buffer at most one frame at a time.", "A timeout shorter than one round-trip propagation delay causes spurious retransmissions even when nothing has been lost.", "Its utilisation rises as the bandwidth-delay product of the link grows.", "It provides flow control as well as error control."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Stop-and-wait is a one-frame pipeline: the sender may have exactly one frame outstanding, so the receiver only ever has to distinguish a new frame from a repeat of the previous one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; only one frame can be in flight, so one buffer position is enough.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; the acknowledgement cannot possibly arrive before one full round trip has elapsed, so a timer set below that bound expires on every successful exchange and the sender retransmits frames that were received correctly.</li><li>(C) False &mdash; a larger bandwidth-delay product means more bits fit in the pipe, and stop-and-wait still sends only one frame per round trip, so utilisation <i>falls</i>.</li><li>(D) True &mdash; the rule that the next frame may not be sent until the previous one is acknowledged is a form of flow control as well as error control.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> (C) reverses the direction of the effect. Pipelining exists precisely because the bandwidth-delay product grows faster than stop-and-wait can fill it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One outstanding frame means one buffer, one sequence bit and a timer that must exceed the round trip, with utilisation that decays as <code>Tp/Tt</code> grows.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q30", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "stop-and-wait-arq", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A sender is connected to its receiver by a 1 Mbps link whose one-way propagation delay is 50 ms. Frames are 1000 bits and acknowledgements are negligible.<br/>What is the smallest number of frames that must be outstanding at all times to keep the link continuously busy?", "options": [], "answer_display": "101", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A link stays busy only while there is always a frame being transmitted. The number of frames that fit in one cycle is the cycle length divided by the transmission time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>Tt = 1000/10<sup>6</sup> = 1 ms</code>.</li><li>One cycle of the protocol is <code>Tt + 2Tp = 1 + 100 = 101 ms</code>.</li><li>In 101 ms the link can carry <code>101/1 = 101</code> frames of this size.</li><li>So a window of 101 frames keeps a frame arriving at the head of the link exactly as the previous one finishes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 101 frames.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the one-way delay (50 ms) instead of the round trip gives 51, and using the bandwidth-delay product in bits (50 000 bits) without dividing by the frame size gives a number 50 times too large. The window is a count of frames, so the cycle must be divided by the frame time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Minimum window for full utilisation <code>= (Tt + 2Tp)/Tt = 1 + 2Tp/Tt</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q31", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "go-back-n-selective-repeat-efficiency", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A selective-repeat sender uses a 3-bit sequence number field. What is the largest window it may use?", "options": ["4", "7", "8", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With <code>m</code>-bit sequence numbers there are <code>2<sup>m</sup></code> distinct numbers. A selective-repeat sender may use at most <code>2<sup>m&minus;1</sup></code> of them, and a Go-Back-N sender at most <code>2<sup>m</sup> &minus; 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Here <code>m = 3</code>, so the sequence space is <code>2<sup>3</sup> = 8</code>.</li><li>Selective repeat halves it: <code>2<sup>2</sup> = 4</code>.</li><li>The reason is that a selective-repeat receiver accepts out-of-order frames, so a retransmission from the previous window must not carry a number that the receiver could confuse with a new frame. A window wider than half the space destroys that separation.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 4.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 7 by recalling the Go-Back-N bound, or 8 by using the whole sequence space. Go-Back-N tolerates a window of <code>2<sup>m</sup>&minus;1</code> only because its receiver refuses out-of-order frames and therefore cannot confuse them.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Keep the pair together: Go-Back-N <code>2<sup>m</sup>&minus;1</code>, selective repeat <code>2<sup>m&minus;1</sup></code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q32", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "go-back-n-selective-repeat-efficiency", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A Go-Back-N receiver has correctly received every frame up to 3. It now receives frame 5. What does it do?", "options": ["It discards frame 5 and sends an acknowledgement for frame 3, the last frame it received in order.", "It buffers frame 5 and delivers it once frame 4 arrives.", "It discards frame 5 and sends a negative acknowledgement asking for frame 4.", "It accepts frame 5, because Go-Back-N tolerates gaps in the sequence."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A standard Go-Back-N receiver has a window of exactly one frame and keeps no reordering buffer. It delivers frames strictly in order and acknowledges cumulatively.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Frame 5 arrives while frame 4 is still missing, so it is out of order.</li><li>With no buffer, the only possible action is to discard it.</li><li>The receiver then repeats the acknowledgement of the highest in-order frame, which is 3.</li><li>The sender, receiving a duplicate acknowledgement for 3, eventually times out and retransmits 4 and 5 &mdash; which is the &lsquo;go back&rsquo; in Go-Back-N.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; discard it and re-acknowledge frame 3.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) describes a selective-repeat receiver, not a Go-Back-N one. Option (C) invents a negative acknowledgement, which standard Go-Back-N does not use.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Go-Back-N: receiver window 1, cumulative acknowledgements, discard out-of-order frames. Selective repeat: receiver buffers, individual acknowledgements.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q33", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "go-back-n-selective-repeat-efficiency", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A Go-Back-N sender uses a window of 10 frames on a 1 Mbps link. Frames are 1000 bits and the round-trip time is 100 ms; acknowledgements are negligible in size. What utilisation does it achieve?", "options": ["50%", "100%", "1.0%", "9.9%"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With pipelining, utilisation is the fraction of the round trip covered by the window: <code>U = N&middot;Tt / (Tt + RTT)</code>, capped at 1.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>Tt = 1000/10<sup>6</sup> = 1 ms</code>.</li><li>The cycle is <code>Tt + RTT = 1 + 100 = 101 ms</code>.</li><li>The window keeps <code>10 &times; 1 = 10 ms</code> of transmission in flight.</li><li><code>U = 10/101 = 0.0990</code>, that is <code>9.9%</code>, below the cap of 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; 9.9%.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing 100% assumes the window always fills the pipe; here it covers only a tenth of the round trip. Choosing 50% or 1.0% comes from mixing the one-way delay with the round trip, or from forgetting the window multiplier.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The window needed for 100% is <code>(Tt + RTT)/Tt = 101</code> frames; ten frames reach a tenth of that.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q34", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "go-back-n-selective-repeat-efficiency", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about Go-Back-N are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The standard Go-Back-N receiver has a window of one frame and no reordering buffer.", "The sender keeps a copy of every frame in its window until that frame is acknowledged.", "A single lost frame can cause a whole window of frames to be retransmitted.", "Go-Back-N can never reach 100% link utilisation, however large its window."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Go-Back-N trades receiver simplicity and bandwidth for sender bookkeeping: the receiver is a one-frame window, the sender holds the unacknowledged window, and a loss costs the whole window.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; that is the defining simplification of Go-Back-N against selective repeat.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; a frame may have to be retransmitted until a cumulative acknowledgement covering it arrives, so it must be retained.</li><li>(C) True &mdash; every frame after the lost one is discarded by the receiver, so all of them are sent again.</li><li>(D) False &mdash; once the window is at least <code>(Tt + RTT)/Tt</code> the link is busy continuously and utilisation reaches 100% in the absence of errors.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> (D) confuses loss recovery with capacity. Losses reduce the achieved goodput, but a sufficiently large window removes the idle time entirely.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Go-Back-N is efficient when losses are rare and wasteful when they are frequent; selective repeat buys back that waste with receiver memory.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q35", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "go-back-n-selective-repeat-efficiency", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A selective-repeat sender runs over a 10 Mbps link with 1250-byte frames and a one-way propagation delay of 30 ms. Acknowledgements are negligible.<br/>What is the smallest window, in frames, that keeps the link continuously busy?", "options": [], "answer_display": "61", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The window must cover one transmission time plus one round trip; that is the number of frames that fit in the pipe.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>Tt = 1250 &times; 8 / 10<sup>7</sup> = 10000/10<sup>7</sup> = 1 ms</code>.</li><li><code>RTT = 2 &times; 30 = 60 ms</code>.</li><li>Cycle <code>= Tt + RTT = 61 ms</code>.</li><li>Frames per cycle <code>= 61/1 = 61</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 61 frames.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using the one-way delay gives 31, which leaves the link idle for half of every round trip. Note also that 61 frames needs a sequence field of at least 7 bits for selective repeat, since <code>2<sup>m&minus;1</sup> &ge; 61</code> requires <code>m = 7</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Window for full utilisation <code>= (Tt + RTT)/Tt</code>; check it against <code>2<sup>m&minus;1</sup></code> before promising it is achievable.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q36", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "pure-and-slotted-aloha", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "What is the maximum throughput of slotted ALOHA, as a fraction of the channel capacity?", "options": ["18.4%", "50%", "36.8%", "100%"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Slotted ALOHA has throughput <code>S = G&middot;e<sup>&minus;G</sup></code>; differentiating gives its peak at <code>G = 1</code>, where <code>S = 1/e &asymp; 0.368</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>S(G) = G e<sup>&minus;G</sup></code>.</li><li><code>dS/dG = e<sup>&minus;G</sup>(1 &minus; G)</code>, which vanishes at <code>G = 1</code>.</li><li><code>S(1) = e<sup>&minus;1</sup> &asymp; 0.368</code>, that is 36.8%.</li><li>This is exactly twice the peak of pure ALOHA, because the vulnerable period is halved.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 36.8%.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 18.4% is the pure-ALOHA figure, and 50% belongs to neither protocol &mdash; it is sometimes quoted for a perfect scheduler with a two-way handshake, not for ALOHA.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Pure <code>1/(2e)</code>, slotted <code>1/e</code>; slotting doubles the ceiling and nothing more.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q37", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "pure-and-slotted-aloha", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In slotted ALOHA, what is the vulnerable period of a frame whose transmission time is <code>T</code>?", "options": ["<code>T/2</code>", "<code>T</code>", "<code>2T</code>", "<code>3T</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The vulnerable period is the span of time during which another station's start of transmission would overlap this frame and destroy it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In slotted ALOHA every station may start only at a slot boundary, and a frame occupies exactly one slot.</li><li>A frame that starts in the same slot as ours collides with it; a frame that starts in the previous slot has already finished, and one that starts in the next slot begins only after ours ends.</li><li>So the only colliding start is our own slot: the vulnerable period is exactly <code>T</code>.</li><li>In pure ALOHA a station may start at any instant, so a start up to <code>T</code> before or after ours also overlaps, giving <code>2T</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; <code>T</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <code>2T</code> by carrying the pure-ALOHA figure across. Slotting is precisely the mechanism that removes the half-frame overlap on either side.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Vulnerable period <code>T</code> &rarr; <code>S = Ge<sup>&minus;G</sup></code>; vulnerable period <code>2T</code> &rarr; <code>S = Ge<sup>&minus;2G</sup></code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q38", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "pure-and-slotted-aloha", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a pure ALOHA network the offered load is <code>G = 0.5</code> frames per frame time. What is the throughput <code>S = G&middot;e<sup>&minus;2G</sup></code>?", "options": ["0.303", "0.184", "0.368", "0.092"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Throughput is the offered load multiplied by the probability that no other transmission overlaps the vulnerable period, which for pure ALOHA is <code>e<sup>&minus;2G</sup></code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>2G = 1</code>.</li><li><code>e<sup>&minus;1</sup> &asymp; 0.3679</code>.</li><li><code>S = 0.5 &times; 0.3679 &asymp; 0.1839</code>.</li><li>That is the maximum of the pure-ALOHA curve, which occurs at <code>G = 0.5</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 0.184.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code>e<sup>&minus;G</sup></code> instead of <code>e<sup>&minus;2G</sup></code> gives 0.303, the slotted-ALOHA value at the same load; 0.368 is the slotted peak, and 0.092 is <code>0.5 &times; e<sup>&minus;3</sup></code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> At the same offered load, slotted ALOHA always delivers more than pure ALOHA, and the gap is a factor of <code>e<sup>G</sup></code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q39", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "pure-and-slotted-aloha", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about ALOHA throughput are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["At <code>G = 1</code> the throughputs of slotted ALOHA and of pure ALOHA are equal.", "Pure ALOHA reaches its peak throughput at <code>G = 0.5</code>.", "Slotted ALOHA reaches its peak throughput at <code>G = 1</code>.", "At its peak, slotted ALOHA carries twice the offered load that pure ALOHA carries at its own peak."], "answer_display": "Options (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two curves are <code>Ge<sup>&minus;2G</sup></code> and <code>Ge<sup>&minus;G</sup></code>; their maxima sit at <code>G = 1/2</code> and <code>G = 1</code> respectively.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) False &mdash; at <code>G = 1</code>, slotted gives <code>0.368</code> while pure gives <code>e<sup>&minus;2</sup> &asymp; 0.135</code>.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; <code>d/dG (Ge<sup>&minus;2G</sup>) = e<sup>&minus;2G</sup>(1 &minus; 2G)</code>, zero at <code>G = 0.5</code>.</li><li>(C) True &mdash; <code>d/dG (Ge<sup>&minus;G</sup>) = e<sup>&minus;G</sup>(1 &minus; G)</code>, zero at <code>G = 1</code>.</li><li>(D) True &mdash; the peaks occur at <code>G = 1</code> and <code>G = 0.5</code>, so the slotted peak carries twice the load for twice the throughput.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (B), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> (A) is the trap: the two curves do cross, but at <code>G = 0</code> and only there &mdash; at every positive load slotted ALOHA is strictly higher.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Peak throughput <code>1/(2e) = 0.184</code> at <code>G = 0.5</code>; <code>1/e = 0.368</code> at <code>G = 1</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q40", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "pure-and-slotted-aloha", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Eight stations share a slotted ALOHA channel. In every slot each station transmits independently with probability <code>1/8</code>.<br/>What is the probability, as a percentage rounded to the nearest integer, that a given slot is successful?", "options": [], "answer_display": "39", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A slot succeeds when exactly one of the eight stations transmits, which is a binomial probability: <code>8 &times; p &times; (1&minus;p)<sup>7</sup></code> with <code>p = 1/8</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The probability that one specified station transmits and the other seven stay silent is <code>(1/8)(7/8)<sup>7</sup></code>.</li><li>Any of the eight stations could be the one, so multiply by 8.</li><li><code>(7/8)<sup>7</sup> = 0.875<sup>7</sup> &asymp; 0.3927</code>.</li><li>As a percentage that is 39.27%, which rounds to 39.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 39 (percent).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 12.5% takes only the single station's own probability and forgets that any of the eight may be the successful one. Reporting 37% forgets that this is a finite population, not the <code>Ge<sup>&minus;G</sup></code> limit; with <code>G = 1</code> the Poisson model gives 36.8% and the binomial gives 39.3%.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For a finite number of stations use the binomial form; the <code>Ge<sup>&minus;G</sup></code> curve is its large-population limit.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q41", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "csma-cd-csma-ca-backoff", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A CSMA/CD station using binary exponential backoff has just suffered its third consecutive collision, counting the first collision as <code>i = 1</code>. From which set does it choose the backoff value <code>k</code>?", "options": ["0 to 3", "0 to 15", "0 to 7", "1 to 8"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> After the <code>i</code>-th collision the station waits <code>k</code> slot times, where <code>k</code> is drawn uniformly from <code>0</code> to <code>2<sup>i</sup> &minus; 1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>i = 3</code>.</li><li><code>2<sup>i</sup> &minus; 1 = 8 &minus; 1 = 7</code>.</li><li>So <code>k</code> ranges over <code>0, 1, ..., 7</code>, that is eight choices.</li><li>The range doubles with each further collision until the implementation's limit is reached.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 0 to 7.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> &ldquo;0 to 3&rdquo; uses <code>2<sup>i&minus;1</sup> &minus; 1</code>, off by one collision; &ldquo;0 to 15&rdquo; is the range after the fourth. Note also that <code>k = 0</code> is allowed, which is why the range is not <code>1 to 8</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Collision <code>i</code> gives <code>2<sup>i</sup></code> slots numbered from 0.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q42", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "csma-cd-csma-ca-backoff", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "What does a CSMA/CD station transmit as soon as it detects a collision?", "options": ["A negative acknowledgement for the damaged frame.", "The frame again, immediately.", "A request-to-send frame.", "A jam signal, which reinforces the collision so that every station on the segment notices it."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A collision is detected by comparing the transmitted signal with the received one. Because the first station to notice may be far from the other participants, it must make the collision last long enough for everyone to see it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The detecting station stops sending the frame and sends a short jam signal instead.</li><li>The jam guarantees that the collision energy persists for at least the slot time, so no station can miss it.</li><li>Every station involved then aborts, enters binary exponential backoff and reschedules.</li><li>Negative acknowledgements belong to error-control protocols at a higher layer, and an immediate retransmission would collide again.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; a jam signal.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that everyone detects the collision at the same moment. Stations are spread along the cable, so the jam exists to equalise what they observe.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Detect &rarr; abort with a jam &rarr; back off &rarr; retry.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q43", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "csma-cd-csma-ca-backoff", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A CSMA/CD LAN has a slot time of 51.2 &micro;s and signals that travel at 2 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s. Treating the slot time as the maximum round-trip propagation delay, what is the greatest cable length the network can span?", "options": ["5120 m", "2560 m", "10240 m", "512 m"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The slot time is twice the maximum one-way propagation delay, <code>2&tau;</code>, because a collision must be able to travel from the far end back to the sender before the sender finishes transmitting.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>2&tau; = 51.2 &micro;s</code>, so <code>&tau; = 25.6 &micro;s</code>.</li><li><code>length = speed &times; &tau; = 2 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> &times; 25.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;6</sup></code>.</li><li><code>= 5120 m</code>.</li><li>Doubling the cable would double <code>&tau;</code> and break the guarantee that the sender is still transmitting when a distant collision is reported.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 5120 m.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 10 240 m uses the slot time as a one-way delay. The slot time is a round trip, and the cable length is fixed by the one-way half of it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> <code>slot time = 2 &times; length / speed</code>; every one of the three quantities follows from the other two.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q44", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "csma-cd-csma-ca-backoff", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about CSMA/CA in IEEE 802.11 are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A data frame is positively acknowledged at the link layer, because a station cannot reliably detect a collision while it is transmitting.", "The RTS/CTS exchange reduces the damage caused by hidden terminals.", "A station that finds the medium busy must wait for it to become idle and then for a further backoff period before transmitting.", "The DIFS is shorter than the SIFS."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CSMA/CA avoids collisions rather than detecting them, because a radio's own transmission drowns out any incoming signal and a hidden terminal is inaudible to the sender.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; with detection impossible, the only way to learn that a frame was lost is to require an explicit acknowledgement and retransmit on its absence.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; the short RTS reserves the medium and the CTS, sent by the receiver, is heard by stations that cannot hear the sender, which is exactly the hidden-terminal case.</li><li>(C) True &mdash; after the medium goes idle a station still defers for a random backoff, so several waiting stations do not all start at the same instant.</li><li>(D) False &mdash; the ordering is <code>SIFS &lt; PIFS &lt; DIFS</code>. The short interframe space gives acknowledgements priority over new frames, which is why it must be shorter.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> (D) reverses the two intervals. Making DIFS shorter than SIFS would let a new data frame jump ahead of the acknowledgement it is waiting for.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Shortest interval wins the contention: SIFS for control responses, DIFS for ordinary data.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q45", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "csma-cd-csma-ca-backoff", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "After four consecutive collisions a CSMA/CD station draws <code>k</code> uniformly at random from <code>0</code> to <code>2<sup>4</sup> &minus; 1</code> and waits <code>k</code> slot times.<br/>What is its expected wait, in slot times?", "options": [], "answer_display": "7.5", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The backoff <code>k</code> is uniform over <code>0 .. 2<sup>i</sup>&minus;1</code>, so its expectation is the midpoint of the range, <code>(2<sup>i</sup> &minus; 1)/2</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code>i = 4</code>, so <code>k</code> is uniform over <code>0, 1, ..., 15</code>, sixteen values.</li><li>The sum is <code>0 + 1 + ... + 15 = 120</code>.</li><li>Expected value <code>= 120/16 = 7.5</code>.</li><li>Equivalently <code>(2<sup>4</sup> &minus; 1)/2 = 15/2 = 7.5</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7.5 slot times.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 8 treats the range as <code>1 .. 16</code> or as <code>0 .. 16</code>; the range is <code>0 .. 2<sup>i</sup>&minus;1</code>, which has an odd maximum and therefore a half-integer mean.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Expected backoff after collision <code>i</code> is <code>(2<sup>i</sup>&minus;1)/2</code> slot times &mdash; it doubles with every further collision.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q46", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "ethernet-standards-switches-bridges", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which address in an arriving frame does a transparent bridge read in order to build its forwarding table?", "options": ["The destination MAC address", "The destination IP address", "The frame's sequence number", "The source MAC address"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A transparent bridge learns by looking at where frames come <i>from</i>: if a frame with source address <code>S</code> arrived on port <code>p</code>, then <code>S</code> is reachable through <code>p</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The frame arrives on a port; the bridge records <code>source &rarr; port</code> in its table, refreshing the entry's age.</li><li>It then looks up the <i>destination</i> address to decide where to forward the frame.</li><li>So the source address is used for learning and the destination address for forwarding &mdash; two different reads for two different purposes.</li><li>IP addresses are not examined at all by a layer-2 bridge.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; the source MAC address.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the address used for learning with the address used for forwarding. Answering &lsquo;destination&rsquo; describes the lookup, not the table construction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Learn from the source, forward by the destination, and flood when the destination is unknown.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q47", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "ethernet-standards-switches-bridges", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "What does a hub do with a frame it receives?", "options": ["It forwards the frame only to the port on which the destination address was learned.", "It discards the frame if the destination address is unknown.", "It repeats the signal out of every other port, because it keeps no address table.", "It rewrites the source address before repeating the frame."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A hub is a physical-layer device: it regenerates and repeats electrical signals and has no notion of a frame, an address or a table.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Bits arriving on one port are re-timed and sent out of all the others.</li><li>There is no filtering, so every station in the collision domain sees every transmission.</li><li>All ports of a hub therefore belong to a single collision domain and a single broadcast domain.</li><li>Only a bridge or switch, which reads addresses, can forward selectively.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; it repeats the signal out of every other port.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (A) describes a switch. Because a hub cannot read an address, it can neither filter nor learn, and it also cannot detect a collision per port &mdash; it merely repeats the collision to everyone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Hub &rarr; repeat everything; switch &rarr; learn, filter and flood.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q48", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "ethernet-standards-switches-bridges", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A switch receives a frame on port 1 whose destination MAC address is one that the switch has learned on port 1 itself. What does the switch do?", "options": ["It filters the frame, that is, drops it, because the destination lies on the same segment as the source.", "It floods the frame out of every other port.", "It forwards the frame out of port 1 as well.", "It returns the frame to the sender with an error indication."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A switch compares the port learned for the destination with the port the frame arrived on. Equal means the two hosts share a segment and no other segment needs the frame.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The source address is learned on port 1, refreshing that entry.</li><li>The destination address is looked up and also resolves to port 1.</li><li>Because the frame is already on the segment where the destination lives, forwarding it anywhere would be wasted bandwidth.</li><li>The switch therefore filters &mdash; drops &mdash; the frame.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; it filters the frame.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a switch always forwards or always floods. Filtering is the third action, and it is what makes a switch a bandwidth-saving device rather than a repeater.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three actions: filter if the destination is on the incoming port, forward if it is on another known port, flood if it is unknown.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q49", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "ethernet-standards-switches-bridges", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about IEEE 802.3 Ethernet are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A frame's payload must be at least 46 bytes for the frame to reach the 64-byte minimum.", "Ethernet delivers frames reliably by acknowledging each one at the data link layer.", "The preamble is seven bytes of alternating 1s and 0s followed by a one-byte start-frame delimiter.", "A switch forwards a broadcast frame out of every port except the one on which it arrived."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Ethernet is a best-effort, connectionless LAN: it detects errors but does not recover from them, and its frame format is fixed by the standard.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></li></ol><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; the minimum frame is 64 bytes and the header plus trailer accounts for 18 bytes (6 + 6 + 2 + 4), so the payload floor is 46 bytes. Shorter payloads are padded.</li><li>(B) False &mdash; there is no link-layer acknowledgement in 802.3. A lost frame is simply lost, and recovery is left to TCP or the application.</li><li>(C) True &mdash; 7 bytes of preamble for clock recovery, then the <code>10101011</code> start-frame delimiter.</li><li>(D) True &mdash; a broadcast address cannot be looked up, so the frame is flooded; the sending port is excluded.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> (B) is the usual error, often borrowed from Wi-Fi, where 802.11 <i>does</i> acknowledge every data frame. Wired Ethernet does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 64-byte minimum, 46-byte payload floor, 18 bytes of overhead, no acknowledgements.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U2-Q50", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "data-link-mac", "subtopic": "ethernet-standards-switches-bridges", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Four switches are cabled so that every pair is joined by exactly one link. The spanning-tree protocol runs and reduces the network to a tree.<br/>How many of those links are left in the blocking state?", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A spanning tree over <code>n</code> nodes has exactly <code>n &minus; 1</code> links. Every other link must be blocked, or a loop would remain.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Four switches, every pair joined: the number of links is <code>C(4,2) = 6</code>.</li><li>A tree on four nodes uses <code>4 &minus; 1 = 3</code> links.</li><li>Blocked links <code>= 6 &minus; 3 = 3</code>.</li><li>Each blocked port still receives bridge protocol data units but forwards no data, so the remaining three links form a loop-free path between every pair.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 links blocked.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 6 minus the number of switches, or blocking links until each switch has degree one. The tree requirement is <code>n&minus;1</code> links in total, not a star.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Blocked links <code>= |E| &minus; (|V| &minus; 1)</code> for a connected graph.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q01", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"1\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 1</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An IPv4 datagram has its IHL (Internet Header Length) field set to 5. What is the length of this header in bytes, and what is the largest header length the field can describe?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">20 bytes; the largest header the field can describe is 60 bytes.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">20 bytes; the largest header the field can describe is 40 bytes.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">5 bytes; the largest header the field can describe is 15 bytes.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">32 bytes; the largest header the field can describe is 60 bytes.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q02", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"2\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 2</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A router receives an IPv4 datagram whose DF (Don't Fragment) bit is set. The MTU of the outgoing link is smaller than the datagram. What does the router do?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">It fragments the datagram into MTU-sized pieces and sets MF on every fragment except the last.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">It discards the datagram and returns ICMP Destination Unreachable with code 4 (fragmentation needed and DF set).</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">It forwards the datagram unchanged, because DF forbids the router from touching the datagram at all.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">It holds the datagram in a queue until a link with a larger MTU becomes free.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q03", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"3\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 3</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An IPv4 datagram carries a 3000-byte payload. It must cross a link whose MTU is 1500 bytes; the standard 20-byte IPv4 header is used and there are no options. Every fragment except the last is filled with the largest multiple of 8 bytes that fits. How many fragments are produced?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 3\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"3\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of fragments.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q04", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"4\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 4</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An IPv4 datagram with a 4000-byte payload is fragmented for an MTU of 1500 bytes (20-byte header, no options). What is the value stored in the fragment offset field of the <b>last</b> fragment?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">185</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">1040</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">370</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">2960</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q05", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"5\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 5</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">The same 4000-byte payload is fragmented for an MTU of 1500 bytes, with a 20-byte header on every fragment. What are the values of the Total Length field of the three fragments, in order?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"5\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q5\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">1500, 1480 and 1040</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"5\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q5\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">1500, 1500 and 1040</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"5\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q5\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">1480, 1480 and 1040</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"5\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q5\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">1500, 1500 and 1060</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q06", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"6\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 6</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about IPv4 fragmentation are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"6\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q6\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">The fragment offset field counts the position of a fragment's payload in units of 8 bytes.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"6\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q6\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">The MF (More Fragments) flag is set in every fragment except the last one.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"6\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q6\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">A fragment produced by one router may be fragmented again by a later router on the path.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"6\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q6\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Reassembly is performed by every router on the path, so the destination always receives a single unfragmented datagram.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q07", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"7\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 7</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A 5000-byte IPv4 payload travels a path whose first link has MTU 1500 bytes and a later link has MTU 576 bytes. A router fragments only when it must, and every fragment except the last is filled with the largest multiple of 8 bytes that fits. The IPv4 header is 20 bytes on every link. How many fragments reach the destination?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 7\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"7\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of fragments.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q08", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "classful-vs-classless-cidr", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"8\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"classful-vs-classless-cidr\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 8</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">classful-vs-classless-cidr</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">The address 172.16.5.9 is interpreted classfully, with no subnetting of the classful network. Which network mask applies?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">255.255.0.0</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">255.0.0.0</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">255.255.255.0</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">255.255.240.0</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q09", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "classful-vs-classless-cidr", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"9\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"classful-vs-classless-cidr\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 9</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">classful-vs-classless-cidr</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A subnet is described by the prefix /27. How many usable host addresses does it contain?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"9\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q9\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">32</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"9\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q9\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">30</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"9\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q9\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">14</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"9\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q9\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">62</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q10", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "classful-vs-classless-cidr", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"10\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"classful-vs-classless-cidr\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 10</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">classful-vs-classless-cidr</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An interface is configured with the address 192.168.10.130/26. What is the network address of its subnet?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"10\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q10\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.10.0</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"10\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q10\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.10.64</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"10\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q10\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.10.128</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"10\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q10\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.10.192</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q11", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "classful-vs-classless-cidr", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"11\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"classful-vs-classless-cidr\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 11</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">classful-vs-classless-cidr</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An interface is configured as 10.20.30.1/23. Which of the following addresses are usable host addresses on that same subnet? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">10.20.31.255</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">10.20.30.255</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">10.20.29.10</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">10.20.31.0</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q12", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "classful-vs-classless-cidr", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"12\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"classful-vs-classless-cidr\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 12</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">classful-vs-classless-cidr</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An ISP is allotted the block 200.10.0.0/18. How many /24 networks does this block contain?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 12\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"12\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of /24 networks.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q13", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "classful-vs-classless-cidr", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"13\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"classful-vs-classless-cidr\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 13</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">classful-vs-classless-cidr</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A router forwards using longest-prefix match against the table below. A packet addressed to <code>10.1.34.19</code> arrives.<br/>10.1.0.0/16 &rarr; R1 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 10.1.32.0/20 &rarr; R2 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 10.1.34.0/24 &rarr; R3 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 0.0.0.0/0 &rarr; R4<br/>Which next hop is chosen?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">R1</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">R3</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">R2</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">R4</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q14", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"14\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 14</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">What is the directed broadcast address of the subnet 192.168.1.64/26?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"14\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q14\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.1.63</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"14\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q14\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.1.64</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"14\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q14\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.1.127</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"14\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q14\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.1.128</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q15", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"15\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 15</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which dotted-decimal subnet mask corresponds to the prefix /28?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">255.255.255.224</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">255.255.255.192</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">255.255.255.240</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">255.255.255.248</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q16", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"16\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 16</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A /16 network is divided into equal subnets, each with the mask 255.255.255.192. How many subnets are created?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 16\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"16\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of subnets.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q17", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"17\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 17</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which single CIDR block summarizes exactly the eight networks 192.168.0.0/24 through 192.168.7.0/24, with no extra address space?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.0.0/21</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.0.0/22</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.0.0/20</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.0.0/23</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q18", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"18\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 18</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">The network 172.16.0.0/16 is divided into equal /20 subnets. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"18\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q18\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Exactly 16 subnets are created.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"18\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q18\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Each subnet has 4094 usable host addresses.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"18\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q18\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">172.16.40.0/20 is a valid subnet boundary of this network.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"18\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q18\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">172.16.32.0/20 is a valid subnet of this network.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q19", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"19\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 19</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Host A is configured as 10.1.2.3/22 and host B as 10.1.6.5/22. Which statement is correct?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">They are on the same subnet, 10.1.0.0/22.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">They are on the same subnet, 10.1.4.0/22.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">They are on different subnets: 10.1.0.0/22 and 10.1.8.0/22.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">They are on different subnets: 10.1.0.0/22 and 10.1.4.0/22.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q20", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"20\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 20</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">The block 203.0.113.0/24 is allocated with VLSM: four departments receive the prefixes /27, /28, /29 and /30 respectively, and no address inside an allocated prefix is left unused. How many addresses of the /24 remain unallocated?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 20\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"20\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of addresses.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q21", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv6-basics-differences", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"21\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv6-basics-differences\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 21</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv6-basics-differences</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">What is the fixed length of the IPv6 base header?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">20 bytes</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">40 bytes</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">48 bytes</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">60 bytes</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q22", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv6-basics-differences", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"22\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv6-basics-differences\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 22</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv6-basics-differences</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which field is present in the IPv4 header but has been removed from the IPv6 base header?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"22\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q22\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Header checksum</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"22\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q22\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Hop Limit</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"22\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q22\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Flow Label</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"22\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q22\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Version</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q23", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv6-basics-differences", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"23\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv6-basics-differences\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 23</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv6-basics-differences</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about IPv6 are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"23\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q23\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">The IPv6 base header carries no header checksum; error detection is delegated to the link and transport layers.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"23\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q23\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">IPv6 routers may fragment a datagram that exceeds the next link's MTU.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"23\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q23\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">The IPv6 base header is 20 bytes long.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"23\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q23\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">IPv6 addresses are 128 bits long.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q24", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv6-basics-differences", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"24\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv6-basics-differences\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 24</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv6-basics-differences</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about IPv6 address notation are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">A single address may contain two separate :: compressions.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">::1 is the loopback address.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">In 2001:db8::1/64, the first 64 bits form the network prefix.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">A single :: may replace one run of consecutive all-zero groups.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q25", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv6-basics-differences", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"25\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv6-basics-differences\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 25</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv6-basics-differences</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An ISP delegates the prefix 2001:db8:abcd::/48 to a customer, who subdivides it into /64 subnets. How many /64 subnets can the customer create?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"25\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q25\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">256</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"25\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q25\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">1024</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"25\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q25\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">4096</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"25\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q25\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">65536</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q26", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"1\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 1</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">As an IPv4 datagram is forwarded by a router, which header field must the router recompute?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Header checksum</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Total Length</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Identification</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Fragment offset</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["Header checksum", "Total Length", "Identification", "Fragment offset"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The IPv4 header checksum covers the header only, and the header is modified in transit because the TTL is decremented at every router.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The router decrements the TTL (Time To Live) field by one on every hop.</li><li>That single change invalidates the checksum, which is computed over the whole header.</li><li>The router therefore recomputes the header checksum before forwarding the datagram.</li><li>Total Length changes only if the datagram is fragmented, and Identification and fragment offset are set once at the source and copied unchanged into every fragment.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; the header checksum.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Total Length is the usual wrong answer, because fragmentation does change it &mdash; but only when a router actually fragments, not on every hop. Identification and fragment offset are never recomputed by a router.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The IPv4 header checksum is a per-hop check that exists only because the TTL changes; IPv6 removed both the field and the recomputation.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q27", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"2\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 2</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">What is the purpose of the 16-bit Identification field in an IPv4 header?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">It carries a sequence number that the receiving transport layer uses to detect loss.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">It tags every fragment of one original datagram so that the destination can group them for reassembly.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">It numbers the fragments in transmission order so that they can be put back in sequence.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">It identifies the sending host uniquely along the path.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["It carries a sequence number that the receiving transport layer uses to detect loss.", "It tags every fragment of one original datagram so that the destination can group them for reassembly.", "It numbers the fragments in transmission order so that they can be put back in sequence.", "It identifies the sending host uniquely along the path."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Reassembly needs two separate pieces of information: which fragments belong to the same original datagram, and where each one sits inside it. The Identification field supplies the first; the fragment offset supplies the second.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>When a router fragments a datagram, every fragment copies the original Identification value.</li><li>The destination collects fragments that share source, destination, protocol and Identification value.</li><li>The fragment offset places each fragment's payload at the correct position, and the MF flag marks the last fragment.</li><li>Ordering is therefore by fragment offset, not by arrival order, which is why option (B) is wrong.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; membership, not position.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is the trap: the field looks like a sequence number. It is not one, because all fragments carry the same Identification value; the position information lives in the fragment offset field.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Identification = membership; fragment offset = position; MF = end marker. All three are needed for reassembly.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q28", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"3\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 3</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An IPv4 datagram carries 12 bytes of options in its header. It must cross a link whose MTU is 1500 bytes. Every fragment except the last carries the largest payload that keeps the fragment offset legal. What is the payload size, in bytes, of each such fragment?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 3\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"3\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of bytes.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "1464", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The MTU bounds the whole datagram, header included, so the payload room per fragment is MTU minus the full header. The payload must also be a multiple of 8 bytes, because the fragment offset counts 8-byte units.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The header is 20 fixed bytes plus 12 bytes of options, so 32 bytes in total. Options are copied into every fragment.</li><li>Payload room = 1500 &minus; 32 = 1468 bytes.</li><li>1468 / 8 = 183.5, which is not a whole number of 8-byte units, so the largest legal payload is 183 &times; 8 = <code>1464 bytes</code>.</li><li>The 4 leftover bytes are simply not used by a non-final fragment.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1464 bytes per fragment.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two errors dominate: using 1480 bytes, which forgets that options are copied into every fragment, and using 1468 bytes, which forgets that the payload must be a multiple of 8.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Payload room = MTU &minus; (20 + options), then round down to a multiple of 8.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q29", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"4\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 4</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A fragment arrives with fragment offset 185 and the MF flag set to 1. At which byte of the original datagram's payload does this fragment's own payload begin?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Byte 185</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Byte 1479</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Byte 1480</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Byte 1488</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["Byte 185", "Byte 1479", "Byte 1480", "Byte 1488"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The fragment offset field counts the position of a fragment's payload in units of 8 bytes, measured from the first byte of the original payload.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>One offset unit = 8 bytes.</li><li>185 units &times; 8 bytes = 1480 bytes.</li><li>Offsets are measured from the first byte of the original payload, which is byte 0.</li><li>So this fragment begins at byte 1480, and MF = 1 tells the destination that more fragments follow.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; byte 1480.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 185 reads the field as a byte count; reporting 1488 adds a spurious 8 bytes by treating the byte numbering as 1-based.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Byte position = fragment offset &times; 8. Convert once, at the start of the calculation.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q30", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"5\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 5</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about IPv4 fragmentation and reassembly are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"5\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q5\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Reassembly is performed only at the final destination; routers forward fragments but never reassemble them.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"5\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q5\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Every fragment produced from one original datagram carries the same Identification value.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"5\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q5\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">A fragment's Total Length field counts that fragment's own IPv4 header as well as its payload.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"5\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q5\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">The fragment offset field is measured in bytes of the original payload.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["Reassembly is performed only at the final destination; routers forward fragments but never reassemble them.", "Every fragment produced from one original datagram carries the same Identification value.", "A fragment's Total Length field counts that fragment's own IPv4 header as well as its payload.", "The fragment offset field is measured in bytes of the original payload."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B) and (C))", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Fragmentation is a source-and-router activity; reassembly is an end-host activity. Each fragment is itself a complete datagram, carrying its own header, so its Total Length includes that header.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; a router that reassembled would have to hold state and would defeat the design; reassembly happens at the destination.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; Identification is copied into every fragment so that the destination can group them.</li><li>(C) True &mdash; each fragment is a datagram in its own right, so Total Length counts header plus payload of that fragment.</li><li>(D) False &mdash; the offset counts 8-byte units, not bytes, which is exactly why every non-final fragment's payload must be a multiple of 8.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (D) is the standard slip: the field is often called a 'byte offset'. It is an offset in 8-byte units, and reading it as bytes understates the position by a factor of eight.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Every fragment is a whole datagram; the three fields that hold the pieces together are Identification, MF and fragment offset.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q31", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"6\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 6</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An IPv4 datagram of total length 4500 bytes (20-byte header, no options) must cross a link whose MTU is 1000 bytes. Every fragment except the last is filled to the largest legal multiple of 8 bytes. How many fragments are produced, and what is the fragment offset of the last fragment?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"6\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q6\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">5 fragments, last offset 3904</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"6\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q6\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">5 fragments, last offset 488</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"6\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q6\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">4 fragments, last offset 488</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"6\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q6\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">6 fragments, last offset 585</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["5 fragments, last offset 3904", "5 fragments, last offset 488", "4 fragments, last offset 488", "6 fragments, last offset 585"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two numbers have to be kept apart: the payload room per fragment, which is MTU minus header rounded down to a multiple of 8, and the fragment offset, which is that payload divided by 8.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Payload to be carried = 4500 &minus; 20 = 4480 bytes.</li><li>Payload room per fragment = 1000 &minus; 20 = 980, rounded down to 976 bytes (976 = 122 &times; 8).</li><li>4480 = 4 &times; 976 + 576, so four full fragments carry 3904 bytes and the last fragment carries the remaining 576 bytes: 5 fragments in total.</li><li>Offsets are 0, 122, 244, 366 and 488, so the last fragment's offset is <code>488</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 5 fragments with the last fragment offset equal to 488.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (A) reports 3904, which is the byte position of the last fragment rather than the value of the offset field; the field counts 8-byte units, so 3904 / 8 = 488. Option (D) comes from treating 1000 bytes as the payload per fragment, which ignores the header.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Count full fragments with 976-byte payloads, then convert the byte position to 8-byte units for the offset field.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q32", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv4-header-format-fragmentation", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"7\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv4-header-format-fragmentation\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 7</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv4-header-format-fragmentation</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A datagram with a 20-byte header carries 3000 bytes of payload and must cross a link with MTU 800 bytes. Every fragment except the last is filled to the largest legal multiple of 8 bytes. Give the fragment offset value, in 8-byte units, of the last fragment.</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 7\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"7\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer (the fragment offset value).</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "291", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The fragment offset of the last fragment equals the number of full fragments multiplied by the payload carried by each, all divided by 8.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Payload room per fragment = 800 &minus; 20 = 780 bytes, rounded down to 776 bytes (776 = 97 &times; 8).</li><li>3000 = 3 &times; 776 + 672, since 3 &times; 776 = 2328 and 3000 &minus; 2328 = 672.</li><li>So there are four fragments, with offsets 0, 97, 194 and 291.</li><li>The last fragment starts 2328 bytes into the original payload, and 2328 / 8 = <code>291</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 291.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 672 gives the size of the last fragment, not its offset; reporting 390 understates the count by using 780 bytes per fragment, which is not a multiple of 8 and therefore illegal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Last offset = (number of full fragments &times; payload per fragment) / 8.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q33", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "classful-vs-classless-cidr", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"8\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"classful-vs-classless-cidr\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 8</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">classful-vs-classless-cidr</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">In classful IPv4 addressing, to which class does the address 240.1.2.3 belong?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Class A</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Class C</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Class D</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Class E</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["Class A", "Class C", "Class D", "Class E"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The class of a classful address is fixed by its first octet: A 0&ndash;127, B 128&ndash;191, C 192&ndash;223, D 224&ndash;239 and E 240&ndash;255.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first octet is 240.</li><li>240 lies in the range 240&ndash;255, which is class E.</li><li>Class D (224&ndash;239) is reserved for multicast, and class E is reserved for experimental use, so neither is assigned to ordinary hosts.</li><li>The remaining three octets play no part in deciding the class.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; Class E.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Class D is the common error, because 224&ndash;239 feels 'high'. The boundary is exact: 239 is the last class D address and 240 is the first class E address.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> The first octet alone decides the class; memorise the five boundaries, not the class names.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q34", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "classful-vs-classless-cidr", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"9\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"classful-vs-classless-cidr\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 9</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">classful-vs-classless-cidr</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">In classful addressing, how many bits of a Class B address form the host portion?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"9\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q9\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">8</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"9\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q9\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">14</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"9\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q9\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">16</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"9\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q9\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">24</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["8", "14", "16", "24"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A classful address is split into a network portion fixed by the class and a host portion made up of everything that is left of the 32 bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A Class B address starts with the bit pattern 10 and uses the first two octets for the network.</li><li>That is 16 network bits, so the default mask is 255.255.0.0, or /16.</li><li>The host portion is therefore 32 &minus; 16 = <code>16 bits</code>.</li><li>16 host bits give 2&#8310;&#8310; &minus; 2 = 65534 usable host addresses per Class B network.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 16 bits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> 14 is the trap: the first two bits of a Class B address are fixed at 10, and candidates sometimes subtract them from the 16 network bits instead of leaving the host bits alone.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Host bits by class: A 24, B 16, C 8. Usable hosts are 2 to that power, minus two.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q35", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "classful-vs-classless-cidr", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"10\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"classful-vs-classless-cidr\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 10</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">classful-vs-classless-cidr</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">The block 192.168.0.0/22 is assigned to a single organisation. How many usable host addresses does the block provide?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 10\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"10\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of addresses.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "1022", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A /p block contains 2^(32&minus;p) addresses, of which the first is the network address and the last is the directed broadcast address, so two are not usable by hosts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Host bits = 32 &minus; 22 = 10.</li><li>Total addresses = 2&#185;&#8304; = 1024.</li><li>Subtract the network address (192.168.0.0) and the directed broadcast address (192.168.3.255).</li><li>Usable addresses = 1024 &minus; 2 = <code>1022</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 1022.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 1024 forgets the two reserved addresses; reporting 1023 forgets only one of them. Both appear as distractors in real papers.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Usable hosts in a /p block = 2^(32&minus;p) &minus; 2 for every prefix shorter than /31.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q36", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "classful-vs-classless-cidr", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"11\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"classful-vs-classless-cidr\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 11</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">classful-vs-classless-cidr</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An organisation owns 172.16.0.0/16 and must carve it into equal subnets, all with the same mask, each supporting at least 4000 usable host addresses. What is the longest prefix that still satisfies the requirement?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">/17</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">/18</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">/19</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">/20</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["/17", "/18", "/19", "/20"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The requirement is on usable hosts, so it is the host bits that must be sized first: 2^h &minus; 2 must be at least the number of hosts required.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>We need 2^h &minus; 2 &ge; 4000, that is 2^h &ge; 4002.</li><li>2&#185;&#185; = 524288, 2&#185;&#8320; = 262144, 2&#185;&#8321; = 2046 usable hosts (too few), 2&#185;&#178; = 4094 usable hosts (enough).</li><li>So h = 12 host bits, and the prefix is 32 &minus; 12 = <code>/20</code>.</li><li>A /19 would also satisfy the requirement but is shorter, and the question asks for the longest prefix that still works.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; /20.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Options (A), (B) and (C) all provide more than 4000 usable hosts, so all three would technically work; the question asks for the longest prefix, which is the smallest block that still fits. Candidates who round 4000 up to 4096 and stop at /19 pick (C) and lose the point.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Size the host bits from usable hosts (2^h &minus; 2), then set prefix = 32 &minus; h.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q37", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "classful-vs-classless-cidr", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"12\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"classful-vs-classless-cidr\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 12</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">classful-vs-classless-cidr</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about classless addressing and CIDR are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"12\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q12\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">A CIDR block is written as address/prefix with any prefix length from /0 to /32, so the old class boundaries no longer constrain the mask.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"12\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q12\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">A /p block contains 2^(32&minus;p) addresses.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"12\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q12\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Classless addressing forbids subnetting a block that has already been subnetted.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"12\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q12\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Several contiguous blocks can be advertised as one aggregate route, which shrinks routing tables.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["A CIDR block is written as address/prefix with any prefix length from /0 to /32, so the old class boundaries no longer constrain the mask.", "A /p block contains 2^(32−p) addresses.", "Classless addressing forbids subnetting a block that has already been subnetted.", "Several contiguous blocks can be advertised as one aggregate route, which shrinks routing tables."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B) and (D))", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> CIDR replaced the fixed class boundaries with an explicit prefix length, which is what makes both arbitrary subdivision and aggregation possible.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; the prefix length is carried with the address, so any value from /0 to /32 is legal.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; the prefix fixes the network bits and the rest are host bits, giving 2^(32&minus;p) addresses.</li><li>(C) False &mdash; a classless block can be subnetted as many times as the owner likes, and that is exactly how VLSM works.</li><li>(D) True &mdash; aggregation of contiguous blocks into a shorter prefix is the main reason CIDR was introduced.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) confuses classless addressing with classful rules: classful addressing did restrict subnetting of an already-subnetted network to a single mask, and CIDR removed that restriction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> CIDR = explicit prefix length + aggregation. Both halves of that sentence are examinable.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q38", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "classful-vs-classless-cidr", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"13\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"classful-vs-classless-cidr\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 13</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">classful-vs-classless-cidr</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A host is configured as 10.10.10.10/12. What is the last usable host address of the subnet it belongs to?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">10.15.255.255</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">10.15.255.254</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">10.255.255.254</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">10.10.255.254</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["10.15.255.255", "10.15.255.254", "10.255.255.254", "10.10.255.254"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For a prefix shorter than /24 the block boundary falls inside the second octet, so the second octet must be masked first; the broadcast address is then the last address of the block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>/12 means a mask of 255.240.0.0, so the block size in the second octet is 16.</li><li>10 AND 240 = 0, so the network address is 10.0.0.0/12.</li><li>The block therefore spans 10.0.0.0 to 10.15.255.255; the last address is the directed broadcast address.</li><li>The last usable host address is one below it: <code>10.15.255.254</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 10.15.255.254.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (A) takes the broadcast address itself, which is never usable; option (C) treats the prefix as /8 and takes the second octet as host bits; option (D) keeps the 10 of the original address in the second octet, which the /12 mask clears.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Mask the octet that contains the boundary first; the broadcast address is the last address of that block, and the last usable host is one below it.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q39", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"14\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 14</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which prefix length corresponds to the subnet mask 255.255.255.224?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"14\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q14\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">/25</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"14\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q14\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">/26</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"14\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q14\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">/27</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"14\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q14\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">/28</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["/25", "/26", "/27", "/28"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A prefix length is the count of leading 1 bits in the mask, so each octet value must be read as a run of 1s followed by 0s.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first three octets are all 255, which is 24 ones.</li><li>224 in binary is 1110 0000, which is three more ones.</li><li>24 + 3 = <code>/27</code>.</li><li>Equivalently, the block size is 256 &minus; 224 = 32, and 32 = 2&#8309; corresponds to 5 host bits, so the prefix is 32 &minus; 5 = 27.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; /27.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> /26 (mask 192) and /28 (mask 240) are the neighbouring values, and candidates who count the 1s in 224 as four rather than three land on /28.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Mask value to prefix: count the 1 bits in the non-255 octet, or use block size = 256 &minus; mask.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q40", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"15\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 15</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An interface is configured as 192.168.5.205/29. What is the subnet (network) address of its subnet?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.5.200</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.5.204</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.5.208</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.5.212</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["192.168.5.200", "192.168.5.204", "192.168.5.208", "192.168.5.212"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A subnet address is obtained by clearing the host bits, which for a prefix inside the last octet means rounding the last octet down to a multiple of the block size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>/29 leaves 3 host bits, so the block size is 2&#179; = 8.</li><li>The subnet boundaries in the last octet are multiples of 8: 192, 200, 208 and so on.</li><li>205 lies between 200 and 207, so the subnet address is <code>192.168.5.200</code>.</li><li>The broadcast address of that subnet is 192.168.5.207, and the usable hosts are 201 to 206.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 192.168.5.200.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) uses a block size of 4, which is a /30; option (C) rounds up to the next multiple of 8 instead of down. Both come from misreading the prefix rather than from an arithmetic slip.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Block size = 2^(32&minus;prefix); the subnet address is the largest multiple of the block size that does not exceed the host address.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q41", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"16\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 16</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">How many /26 subnets can be carved out of the block 10.0.0.0/22?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 16\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"16\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of subnets.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "16", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Subdividing a /p block into /q subnets uses q &minus; p extra network bits, which yields 2^(q&minus;p) subnets of equal size.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The given block is /22 and the target subnet is /26.</li><li>Extra network bits borrowed = 26 &minus; 22 = 4.</li><li>Number of subnets = 2&#8308; = <code>16</code>.</li><li>Each subnet then holds 2&#8310; = 64 addresses, and 16 &times; 64 = 1024, which is exactly the size of a /22 block.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 16 subnets.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 4 counts the borrowed bits rather than the subnets; reporting 64 confuses the number of subnets with the number of addresses inside one subnet.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Subnets = 2^(new prefix &minus; old prefix). Check the total addresses match as a sanity test.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q42", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"17\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 17</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which pair of /25 blocks aggregates into exactly one /24 with no extra address space?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">200.1.2.0/25 and 200.1.2.128/25</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">200.1.2.0/25 and 200.1.3.0/25</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">200.1.2.128/25 and 200.1.3.0/25</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">200.1.2.0/25 and 200.1.2.192/25</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["200.1.2.0/25 and 200.1.2.128/25", "200.1.2.0/25 and 200.1.3.0/25", "200.1.2.128/25 and 200.1.3.0/25", "200.1.2.0/25 and 200.1.2.192/25"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two blocks aggregate exactly when they are the two halves of one larger block: they must share every prefix bit above the aggregate length and differ only in the bit that the aggregate fixes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A /24 splits into exactly two /25 halves: the one with bit 25 equal to 0 and the one with bit 25 equal to 1.</li><li>For 200.1.2.0/24 those halves are 200.1.2.0/25 (addresses .0 to .127) and 200.1.2.128/25 (addresses .128 to .255).</li><li>Their union is the whole /24, so they aggregate with no waste.</li><li>Option (B) and option (C) each span two different /24 blocks and cannot be summarised by one /24; option (D) pairs a valid /25 with 200.1.2.192/25, which is not even a legal /25 boundary because 192 is a /26 boundary.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Any two /25s feel summarisable, but the blocks must be siblings. A pair straddling two /24s would leave a hole, so the aggregate would have to be a /23 covering address space the organisation does not own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Aggregate only siblings: identical prefix bits above the aggregate length, differing only in the bits the aggregate fixes.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q43", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"18\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 18</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about VLSM and supernetting are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"18\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q18\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">VLSM allows the subnets carved from one address block to use different prefix lengths.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"18\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q18\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">In a VLSM design, allocating the largest subnets first keeps the remaining space contiguous and usable.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"18\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q18\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Every subnet in a VLSM design must use the same mask.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"18\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q18\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Route summarisation works best when the subnets of a block are contiguous and aligned to their own block sizes.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["VLSM allows the subnets carved from one address block to use different prefix lengths.", "In a VLSM design, allocating the largest subnets first keeps the remaining space contiguous and usable.", "Every subnet in a VLSM design must use the same mask.", "Route summarisation works best when the subnets of a block are contiguous and aligned to their own block sizes."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B) and (D))", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> VLSM is the freedom to subnet a block repeatedly with different prefix lengths, and its practical success depends on allocating in descending size order so that no large block is split by a small one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; that freedom is the definition of VLSM.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; a small subnet placed inside a large block's address range destroys that block's alignment, so the large ones must be placed first.</li><li>(C) False &mdash; a single mask is fixed-length subnetting, which is precisely what VLSM replaces.</li><li>(D) True &mdash; contiguous, aligned blocks share prefix bits above the aggregate length, which is the condition for exact summarisation.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) restates fixed-length subnetting and is the usual error, because many candidates learn subnetting as 'all subnets get the same mask' before meeting VLSM.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> VLSM = different masks in one block; allocate largest first; aggregate only contiguous, aligned blocks.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q44", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "subnet-masking-supernetting", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"19\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"subnet-masking-supernetting\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 19</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">subnet-masking-supernetting</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">The block 172.16.0.0/16 must be divided into equal subnets, all with the same mask, each providing at least 500 usable host addresses. What is the largest number of such subnets?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">128</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">256</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">512</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">1024</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["128", "256", "512", "1024"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The host requirement fixes the subnet size, and the subnet size fixes how many subnets fit in the parent block; maximising the count means choosing the smallest subnet that still meets the host requirement.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>We need 2^h &minus; 2 &ge; 500, that is 2^h &ge; 502.</li><li>2&#8313; = 512, giving 510 usable hosts, so h = 9 and the subnet prefix is 32 &minus; 9 = /23.</li><li>The number of /23 subnets in a /16 block is 2^(23&minus;16) = 2&#8311; = <code>128</code>.</li><li>A /24 subnet would give only 254 usable hosts, too few; a /22 would give more than needed but only 64 subnets.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) &mdash; 128 subnets.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is the /24 count, and a /24 gives only 254 usable hosts, below the 500 required, so it cannot be used at all. Option (D) comes from sizing to a /22 and then counting its subnets.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Smallest legal subnet gives the largest count: solve 2^h &minus; 2 &ge; hosts, then count subnets as 2^(q &minus; p).</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q45", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv6-basics-differences", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"20\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv6-basics-differences\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 20</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv6-basics-differences</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">The IPv6 Next Header field carries out the job of which IPv4 header field?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"20\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q20\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Total Length</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"20\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q20\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Header checksum</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"20\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q20\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Identification</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"20\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q20\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Protocol</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["Total Length", "Header checksum", "Identification", "Protocol"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> IPv6 keeps one field whose job is to name what follows the current header. In the base header that is the upper-layer protocol; in an extension header it is the next extension header.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In IPv4, the Protocol field holds a number such as 6 for TCP or 17 for UDP.</li><li>In IPv6 the same number appears in the Next Header field of the base header.</li><li>Unlike IPv4, the chain can continue: if the Next Header value names an extension header, that header carries its own Next Header field pointing further on.</li><li>So Next Header generalises the Protocol field rather than replacing its meaning.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D) &mdash; Protocol.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is a frequent answer because IPv6's header differences are usually taught as a list of removals; the checksum was removed, not replaced by Next Header.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Next Header = IPv4 Protocol, extended into a chain through optional extension headers.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q46", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv6-basics-differences", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"21\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv6-basics-differences\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 21</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv6-basics-differences</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following is the correctly compressed form of 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0001?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">2001:db8:0:0:0:0:0:1::</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">2001:db8:1::</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">2001:db8::1</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">2001:db8:0000:0:0:0:0:1</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["2001:db8:0:0:0:0:0:1::", "2001:db8:1::", "2001:db8::1", "2001:db8:0000:0:0:0:0:1"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two rules govern IPv6 text compression: leading zeros inside a group are dropped, and one run of all-zero groups may be replaced by a double colon, at most once.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Dropping leading zeros in each group gives 2001:db8:0:0:0:0:0:1.</li><li>The longest run of all-zero groups is five groups long (the third through seventh).</li><li>Replacing that run with <code>::</code> gives 2001:db8::1.</li><li>The double colon may appear only once, so option (A) is illegal; option (C) moves 1 into the wrong group, which changes the address; option (D) keeps leading zeros and compresses nothing.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C) &mdash; 2001:db8::1.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is the trap for candidates who count groups from the wrong end: 2001:db8:1:: expands to 2001:0db8:0001:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000, a completely different address.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Drop leading zeros; compress exactly one run of zero groups; never write :: twice.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q47", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv6-basics-differences", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"22\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv6-basics-differences\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 22</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv6-basics-differences</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A fully expanded IPv6 address is written as eight groups of four hexadecimal digits. How many hexadecimal digits does the address contain in total?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 22\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"22\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of hexadecimal digits.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "32", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The length of the address in digits follows directly from its structure: eight groups, four digits each, and each hexadecimal digit carries four bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Groups = 8, digits per group = 4.</li><li>Total digits = 8 &times; 4 = <code>32</code>.</li><li>Each hexadecimal digit encodes 4 bits, so 32 &times; 4 = 128 bits, which is the size of an IPv6 address.</li><li>Compression with :: and the dropping of leading zeros change how the address is written, never how many digits it stands for.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 32 hexadecimal digits.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 8 counts the groups; reporting 16 halves the true figure. The check is that 32 digits &times; 4 bits = 128 bits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> 8 groups &times; 4 digits = 32 hex digits = 128 bits. Any of the three forms can be converted into the others.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q48", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv6-basics-differences", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"23\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv6-basics-differences\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 23</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv6-basics-differences</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about the IPv6 header and its handling are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"23\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q23\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">IPv6 routers do not fragment packets; only the source host fragments.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"23\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q23\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">The IPv6 base header has no checksum, so a router does not recompute a checksum after decrementing the hop limit.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"23\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q23\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">IPv6 resolves link-layer addresses with ARP, exactly as IPv4 does.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"23\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q23\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">IPv6 options are carried in extension headers, which is why the base header keeps a fixed length of 40 bytes.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["IPv6 routers do not fragment packets; only the source host fragments.", "The IPv6 base header has no checksum, so a router does not recompute a checksum after decrementing the hop limit.", "IPv6 resolves link-layer addresses with ARP, exactly as IPv4 does.", "IPv6 options are carried in extension headers, which is why the base header keeps a fixed length of 40 bytes."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B) and (D))", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three design decisions distinguish the IPv6 base header: fragmentation is a source-only operation, the header checksum was removed, and optional information moved into a chain of extension headers.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; a router that receives an oversized packet returns ICMPv6 Packet Too Big so that the source can reduce its size; routers never fragment.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; with no checksum in the base header there is nothing to recompute, which is one reason IPv6 forwarding is cheaper than IPv4 forwarding.</li><li>(C) False &mdash; IPv6 uses the Neighbour Discovery Protocol, part of ICMPv6, with multicast solicitations and advertisements instead of broadcast ARP.</li><li>(D) True &mdash; because options live in extension headers, every base header is exactly 40 bytes, so routers can process it with fixed offsets.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (C) is the standard error: ARP is an IPv4 protocol that depends on broadcast, and IPv6 deliberately replaced it with NDP over multicast.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> IPv6 base header: fixed 40 bytes, no checksum, no router fragmentation, options in extension headers, NDP instead of ARP.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q49", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv6-basics-differences", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"24\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv6-basics-differences\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 24</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv6-basics-differences</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about IPv6 addressing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">The symbol :: may replace exactly one run of one or more all-zero groups, and it may appear at most once in an address.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">The unspecified address :: and the loopback address ::1 are two different addresses.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">A link-local IPv6 address begins with fe80::/10.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">An IPv6 address is written as eight groups of four hexadecimal digits, and a group may never be shortened by dropping its leading zeros.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["The symbol :: may replace exactly one run of one or more all-zero groups, and it may appear at most once in an address.", "The unspecified address :: and the loopback address ::1 are two different addresses.", "A link-local IPv6 address begins with fe80::/10.", "An IPv6 address is written as eight groups of four hexadecimal digits, and a group may never be shortened by dropping its leading zeros."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B) and (C))", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> IPv6 text notation has a small set of rules, and each one is separately examinable: how :: works, which special addresses exist, and what the standard prefixes are.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True &mdash; :: stands for a run of zero groups, and a second :: would make the expansion ambiguous, so at most one is allowed.</li><li>(B) True &mdash; :: is the unspecified address (all zeros, used before an address is known) and ::1 is the loopback address; they are distinct.</li><li>(C) True &mdash; fe80::/10 is the link-local prefix, valid only on a single link and never routed.</li><li>(D) False &mdash; dropping leading zeros inside a group is required by the canonical text form, so 0db8 is written db8.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (D) reverses a real rule: leading zeros must be dropped, and only trailing zeros inside a group are kept. Candidates who confuse 'drop leading zeros' with 'never drop zeros' pick it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One :: per address; :: and ::1 differ; fe80::/10 is link-local; leading zeros inside a group are dropped.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U3-Q50", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "ipv4-subnetting", "subtopic": "ipv6-basics-differences", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"25\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"ipv4-subnetting\" data-unit-number=\"3\" data-subtopic=\"ipv6-basics-differences\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 25</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">ipv6-basics-differences</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An IPv6 packet consists of the 40-byte base header, one 16-byte extension header and 1200 bytes of upper-layer data. What value does the Payload Length field of the base header hold?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"25\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q25\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">1200</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"25\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q25\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">1216</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"25\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q25\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">1240</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"25\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q25\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">1256</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div>", "options": ["1200", "1216", "1240", "1256"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The IPv6 Payload Length field counts everything that follows the 40-byte base header, which means extension headers are included in it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The base header is excluded from the count, and it is always 40 bytes.</li><li>Everything after it is counted: the 16-byte extension header plus the 1200 bytes of upper-layer data.</li><li>Payload Length = 16 + 1200 = <code>1216</code>.</li><li>The whole packet is 40 + 1216 = 1256 bytes, which is a different quantity and is not what the field stores.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) &mdash; 1216.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (A) omits the extension header, because extension headers feel like 'header' rather than 'payload'. Option (D) is the total packet length, the IPv4 Total Length idea carried over by mistake.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> IPv6 Payload Length = everything after the 40-byte base header, extension headers included; total packet length is not stored anywhere.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q01", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"1\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 1</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">In distance-vector routing a router learns a destination only from the vectors its neighbours advertise. Which one of the following is the direct cause of the <b>count-to-infinity</b> problem?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">A router advertises a route it learned from a neighbour back to that same neighbour, so a failed route keeps looking reachable.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">A router floods its complete link-state database to every other router in the network.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Two routers compute different shortest-path trees from the same topology database.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"1\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q1\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">The TTL of an advertisement expires before the advertisement reaches the far end of the network.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["A router advertises a route it learned from a neighbour back to that same neighbour, so a failed route keeps looking reachable.", "A router floods its complete link-state database to every other router in the network.", "Two routers compute different shortest-path trees from the same topology database.", "The TTL of an advertisement expires before the advertisement reaches the far end of the network."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A distance-vector router holds only <i>distances</i>, not paths. When neighbour B advertises “network N is 2 hops away”, A cannot tell whether B's route to N runs through A itself. If it does, the two routers can keep quoting each other's stale distance and the metric climbs slowly towards infinity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A—B—C in a line, with N attached to A. Both A and B hold a route to N.</li><li>The A—N link fails. A marks N as unreachable (distance 16 in RIP).</li><li>Before A's next advertisement, B advertises “N is 2 away”, a distance B learned <i>through A</i>.</li><li>A now believes N is reachable through B at 3, B then hears 3 and believes 4, and so on.</li><li>The distance climbs one step per exchange until it reaches the metric's infinity (16 in RIP), long after the network has actually lost N. That is counting to infinity.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — a route reflected back to the neighbour it came from keeps a dead route alive.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes link-state flooding, which is the opposite design: every router builds the full topology and never depends on a neighbour's claim about a path.</li><li>(C) is not a defect of the protocol at all; two routers running the same database and the same algorithm agree.</li><li>(D) confuses a routing advertisement with an IP packet. Routing updates are not forwarded hop by hop with a TTL that can expire and cause a counting loop.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Students blame “slow convergence”. Slow convergence is the <i>symptom</i>; the cause is that a router cannot distinguish a route through a neighbour from a route through itself, because the vector carries no path information.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Distance-vector loops arise from <i>path ignorance</i>. Every cure — split horizon, poisoned reverse, hold-down, triggered updates, path vectors — adds either path information or a rule that stops a router believing a neighbour's stale claim.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q02", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"2\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 2</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">In a link-state protocol such as OSPF, what does a router do when it detects that one of its directly connected links has changed state?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">It reliably floods a new link-state advertisement describing the change to every router in the area, and each router recomputes shortest paths with Dijkstra's algorithm.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">It sends its entire routing table to its two neighbours, which pass the table on to their neighbours.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">It waits for the next periodic update, 30 seconds later, and reports the change then.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"2\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q2\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">It reports the change only to the designated router, which stores it until another router asks for a topology copy.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["It reliably floods a new link-state advertisement describing the change to every router in the area, and each router recomputes shortest paths with Dijkstra's algorithm.", "It sends its entire routing table to its two neighbours, which pass the table on to their neighbours.", "It waits for the next periodic update, 30 seconds later, and reports the change then.", "It reports the change only to the designated router, which stores it until another router asks for a topology copy."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Link-state routing separates <i>dissemination</i> from <i>computation</i>. Dissemination is reliable flooding of link-state advertisements (LSAs); computation is Dijkstra's algorithm run locally on the resulting database. A change is therefore reported as an event, not as a table exchange.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The interface detects the change (carrier loss, hello timeout, or a new neighbour).</li><li>The router originates a fresh LSA carrying a higher sequence number and floods it to all neighbours, who acknowledge, re-flood and so reach every router in the area.</li><li>Each router stores the LSA in its link-state database, replacing the older copy.</li><li>Each router then re-runs Dijkstra on the updated database and installs the new shortest-path tree.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — flood the new LSA, then recompute locally.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is distance-vector behaviour: neighbours receive distances, not topology, and they cannot recompute a shortest path from a table they do not have.</li><li>(C) OSPF does refresh LSAs periodically, but a topology change is flooded at once; waiting 30 s would leave the network forwarding on a stale tree.</li><li>(D) There is no query-response topology service in OSPF. The designated router reduces adjacencies on a broadcast LAN; it is not a repository that other routers poll.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that link-state means “no periodic traffic”. OSPF sends hello packets and refreshes LSAs, but neither is used to <i>discover</i> topology changes — the event-driven LSA does that.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Link-state = flood the fact, let everyone compute. Distance-vector = tell the neighbours the answer, let them trust it.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q03", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "bgp-and-path-vector", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"3\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"bgp-and-path-vector\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 3</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">bgp-and-path-vector</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which one of the following describes BGP correctly?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"3\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q3\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">A path-vector protocol that runs over TCP port 179 and advertises the list of autonomous systems (AS_PATH) a route has crossed.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"3\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q3\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">A distance-vector protocol that runs over UDP port 520 and counts hops, with 16 meaning infinity.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"3\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q3\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">A link-state protocol that floods link-state advertisements and runs Dijkstra inside each autonomous system.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"3\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q3\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">A transport-layer protocol that guarantees delivery of routing updates by retransmitting them at the IP layer.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["A path-vector protocol that runs over TCP port 179 and advertises the list of autonomous systems (AS_PATH) a route has crossed.", "A distance-vector protocol that runs over UDP port 520 and counts hops, with 16 meaning infinity.", "A link-state protocol that floods link-state advertisements and runs Dijkstra inside each autonomous system.", "A transport-layer protocol that guarantees delivery of routing updates by retransmitting them at the IP layer."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> BGP is the inter-domain routing protocol of the Internet. It carries the <code class=\"mm\">AS_PATH</code> attribute — the sequence of autonomous systems a route has traversed — which makes it a <i>path-vector</i> protocol: a router can see the whole AS-level path, not just a distance.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>BGP peers form a TCP connection on port 179; reliability, ordering and retransmission come from TCP, not from BGP itself.</li><li>Each update carries a prefix plus path attributes: AS_PATH, NEXT_HOP, LOCAL_PREF, MED, ORIGIN and others.</li><li>A speaker prepends its own AS number when advertising the route to an external peer, so AS_PATH records the path and doubles as the loop detector: seeing your own AS in the path means discard the route.</li><li>Selection is policy-driven and lexicographic, not a single shortest-path computation.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — path vector over TCP 179, advertising AS_PATH.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) is RIP: UDP 520, hop count, 16 as infinity, inside one domain.</li><li>(C) is OSPF or IS-IS, which are intra-domain link-state protocols.</li><li>(D) BGP is an application-layer protocol riding on TCP; it does not itself retransmit at the IP layer.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calling BGP “distance vector with AS numbers”. AS_PATH carries the actual sequence of systems, which is precisely what a distance vector lacks; that is why BGP does not suffer count-to-infinity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Three protocols, three shapes: RIP = distance vector, OSPF = link state, BGP = path vector over TCP 179.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q04", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"4\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 4</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Host X (192.168.1.10/24) must send an IP packet to host Y (192.168.1.20/24). X knows Y's IP address but not its MAC address. How is the ARP request carried on the LAN?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">In a frame whose destination MAC address is FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF, so that every station on the LAN receives it.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">In a frame unicast to the default gateway's MAC address.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">In a frame unicast to Y's MAC address, which X reads from the destination IP header.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"4\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q4\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">In a frame with destination MAC 01:00:5E:00:00:01, the all-routers multicast address.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["In a frame whose destination MAC address is FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF, so that every station on the LAN receives it.", "In a frame unicast to the default gateway's MAC address.", "In a frame unicast to Y's MAC address, which X reads from the destination IP header.", "In a frame with destination MAC 01:00:5E:00:00:01, the all-routers multicast address."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> ARP bridges the gap between the network layer and the link layer: it maps an IP address to a MAC address on the <i>same</i> link. Because X does not yet know the target's MAC address, the request must reach every station, so it is broadcast at the link layer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>X checks its ARP cache for 192.168.1.20. There is no entry.</li><li>X builds an ARP request (“who has 192.168.1.20? tell 192.168.1.10”) and encapsulates it in an Ethernet frame with destination FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF.</li><li>Every station on the LAN receives the frame; only Y recognises its own IP address.</li><li>Y replies with its MAC address, normally as a <i>unicast</i> frame to X, and X caches the binding.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — the ARP request is link-layer broadcast.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) The gateway is irrelevant: Y is on the same subnet, so no router is involved.</li><li>(C) X cannot unicast to a MAC address it does not know — that is the whole reason for the request.</li><li>(D) 01:00:5E:00:00:01 is an IPv4 multicast mapping, used after multicast group membership, not for ARP.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming ARP is always broadcast. Only the <i>request</i> is broadcast; the reply is usually unicast, and both ends cache the result for a few minutes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Same subnet → ARP for the destination. Different subnet → ARP for the <i>gateway</i>, and the packet keeps the remote destination IP inside the frame.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q05", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"5\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 5</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Five routers R1–R5 are connected in a line, R1—R2—R3—R4—R5, and every link costs 1. At the start each router knows only its own directly connected links. All routers then exchange their complete distance vectors simultaneously, once per round, in the style of RIP.<br/>After how many rounds does R1 first hold a finite distance to R5?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 5\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"5\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of rounds.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A distance-vector network propagates one hop of new information per exchange round, because a router can only learn what its neighbours already knew at the start of that round.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Round 0: R1 knows R2 (1); R2 knows R1 and R3; R3 knows R2 and R4; R4 knows R3 and R5; R5 knows R4.</li><li>Round 1: R3 learns R5 = 2 from R4; R2 learns R4 = 2 from R3; R1 learns R3 = 2 from R2.</li><li>Round 2: R2 learns R5 = 3 from R3 (2 + 1); R1 learns R4 = 3 from R2.</li><li>Round 3: R1 learns R5 = 4 from R2 (3 + 1). This is the first round in which R1 has a finite distance to R5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 rounds.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 by counting the hops instead of the rounds. R1 is four hops from R5, but information travels one hop per round and R1 already knows its own link to R2 at round 0, so it needs three rounds, not four.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> In a line of <code class=\"mm\">k</code> links, the end router learns the far end after <code class=\"mm\">k − 1</code> rounds, and the table is fully converged after <code class=\"mm\">k − 1</code> rounds of simultaneous exchange.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q06", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"6\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 6</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A host with no IP address boots and obtains one from a DHCP server. Which sequence of DHCP messages is correct?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"6\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q6\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">DHCPDISCOVER (broadcast) → DHCPOFFER → DHCPREQUEST (broadcast) → DHCPACK</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"6\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q6\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">DHCPOFFER → DHCPDISCOVER → DHCPACK → DHCPREQUEST</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"6\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q6\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">DHCPREQUEST → DHCPACK → DHCPDISCOVER → DHCPOFFER</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"6\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q6\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">DHCPDISCOVER → DHCPREQUEST → DHCPOFFER → DHCPACK</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["DHCPDISCOVER (broadcast) → DHCPOFFER → DHCPREQUEST (broadcast) → DHCPACK", "DHCPOFFER → DHCPDISCOVER → DHCPACK → DHCPREQUEST", "DHCPREQUEST → DHCPACK → DHCPDISCOVER → DHCPOFFER", "DHCPDISCOVER → DHCPREQUEST → DHCPOFFER → DHCPACK"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> DHCP uses four messages, remembered as <b>DORA</b>: Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge. The client has no address at the start, so the first two messages cannot be unicast to the client's own IP address.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The client broadcasts DHCPDISCOVER, since it knows neither its own address nor the server's.</li><li>One or more servers answer with DHCPOFFER, each proposing an address and lease terms.</li><li>The client broadcasts DHCPREQUEST naming the offer it accepts; the broadcast also tells any other offering server that its offer was declined.</li><li>The chosen server replies with DHCPACK, which confirms the address, mask, gateway, DNS servers and lease duration.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B), (C) and (D) all reorder the four messages; the server cannot offer before it has been asked, and it cannot acknowledge before the client has chosen an offer.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing DHCPREQUEST is unicast to the chosen server. It is broadcast, precisely so that the servers whose offers were not taken can release those addresses.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> DORA: Discover (broadcast), Offer, Request (broadcast), Ack. Client port 68, server port 67, both UDP.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q07", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"7\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 7</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about link-state routing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"7\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q7\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Once flooding has finished, every router in an area holds the same topology database, so their shortest-path trees agree.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"7\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q7\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Each router runs Dijkstra's algorithm on the topology database to build its own shortest-path tree.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"7\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q7\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">A router sends its complete routing table to each neighbour every 30 seconds.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"7\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q7\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">A topology change is normally reported immediately, by a new advertisement, rather than at the next periodic timer.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["Once flooding has finished, every router in an area holds the same topology database, so their shortest-path trees agree.", "Each router runs Dijkstra's algorithm on the topology database to build its own shortest-path tree.", "A router sends its complete routing table to each neighbour every 30 seconds.", "A topology change is normally reported immediately, by a new advertisement, rather than at the next periodic timer."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Link-state routing gives every router the same <i>facts</i> (the flooded database) and then lets each router compute its own <i>answers</i> (its shortest-path tree) locally.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Flooding is reliable and the LSAs are identical everywhere, so the databases converge and every router's tree is computed from the same graph.</li><li>(B) True. That local computation is the defining step of the link-state design.</li><li>(C) False. Sending a full routing table to neighbours every 30 seconds is RIP's behaviour, a distance-vector protocol. Link-state routers send small LSAs describing their own links.</li><li>(D) True. Event-driven flooding is what makes link-state converge quickly after a failure.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(C) is the distance-vector periodic table exchange, the design link-state routing was invented to replace.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking each router's tree is the same. The <i>database</i> is identical; the <i>tree</i> is rooted at each router separately, so the trees differ.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Same database, different roots. Flood the facts once, compute locally many times.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q08", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"8\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 8</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Under split horizon with poisoned reverse, what does a router send back to the neighbour from which it learned a route to network N?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">The route to N with the metric set to infinity — an explicit “unreachable” advertisement.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Nothing at all for N; the entry is simply left out of the update.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">The same metric it holds for N, so the neighbour can confirm the route is still valid.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"8\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q8\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">The metric for N increased by one, to force the neighbour to recompute.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["The route to N with the metric set to infinity — an explicit “unreachable” advertisement.", "Nothing at all for N; the entry is simply left out of the update.", "The same metric it holds for N, so the neighbour can confirm the route is still valid.", "The metric for N increased by one, to force the neighbour to recompute."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Both split horizon and poisoned reverse stop a router reflecting a route back to the neighbour that supplied it. They differ in <i>how</i>: plain split horizon omits the entry, poisoned reverse includes it with metric infinity.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A learned “N is 2 away” from B, so B is A's next hop for N.</li><li>When A advertises to B, plain split horizon leaves N out entirely.</li><li>Poisoned reverse instead advertises N with the metric set to 16 (RIP's infinity), explicitly saying “do not reach N through me”.</li><li>The explicit infinity is stronger: it cancels any older, smaller value still sitting in B's table from an earlier exchange, which omission alone cannot do.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — the route is advertised back with an infinite metric.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) describes split horizon <i>without</i> poisoned reverse.</li><li>(C) is the behaviour that causes the loop in the first place.</li><li>(D) no protocol adds one to a metric for this purpose; that would make a dead route look alive.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the two mechanisms as identical. Omission can leave a stale entry untouched; an explicit infinity overwrites it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Split horizon = say nothing. Poisoned reverse = say infinity. Neither cures loops that involve three or more routers.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q09", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"9\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 9</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An OSPF area has these links, each with the stated cost: A–B 2, A–C 5, B–C 1, B–D 6, C–D 3, C–E 8, D–E 2.<br/>Running Dijkstra from router A, what is the total cost of the least-cost path from A to E?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 9\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"9\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer cost.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Dijkstra grows a set of settled nodes in order of increasing distance. At each step the unsettled node with the smallest tentative distance is finalised, and its neighbours are relaxed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Settle A (0). Relax: B = 2, C = 5.</li><li>Settle B (2). Relax through B: C = min(5, 2+1) = 3; D = 2+6 = 8.</li><li>Settle C (3). Relax through C: D = min(8, 3+3) = 6; E = 3+8 = 11.</li><li>Settle D (6). Relax through D: E = min(11, 6+2) = 8.</li><li>Settle E (8). The least-cost path is A→B→C→D→E with cost 2 + 1 + 3 + 2 = 8.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Taking the fewest hops: A→C→E is two hops but costs 5 + 8 = 13, and A→B→D→E is three hops at 2 + 6 + 2 = 10. OSPF minimises the sum of costs, not the number of hops.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Run Dijkstra by settling, not by eyeballing. Each settlement finalises one distance and relaxes only that node's edges.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q10", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "bgp-and-path-vector", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"10\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"bgp-and-path-vector\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 10</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">bgp-and-path-vector</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Two routers in the same autonomous system, AS 100, exchange routes over an iBGP session. When router R advertises a route it learned from an external peer, what does the AS_PATH in the iBGP advertisement look like?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"10\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q10\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Unchanged — iBGP does not prepend the local AS; only an eBGP advertisement does.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"10\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q10\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Prefixed with 100, because every BGP speaker prepends its own AS number.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"10\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q10\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Prefixed with the peer's AS twice, to make the route less attractive to other speakers.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"10\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q10\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Emptied, because iBGP routes carry only the NEXT_HOP attribute.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["Unchanged — iBGP does not prepend the local AS; only an eBGP advertisement does.", "Prefixed with 100, because every BGP speaker prepends its own AS number.", "Prefixed with the peer's AS twice, to make the route less attractive to other speakers.", "Emptied, because iBGP routes carry only the NEXT_HOP attribute."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> AS_PATH records the autonomous systems a route has <i>crossed</i>. A prefix is added when the route is exported to a different AS (eBGP). Inside an AS the path has not changed, so iBGP advertisements leave AS_PATH untouched.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>R receives an eBGP update from AS 200 with AS_PATH “200 300”.</li><li>R selects it and advertises it to its iBGP peers in AS 100; the AS_PATH is still “200 300”.</li><li>When R, or any speaker in AS 100, advertises the route to a peer in another AS, it prepends 100, giving “100 200 300”.</li><li>Hence inside the AS the attribute is unchanged; the local AS appears only on export.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — iBGP does not prepend the local AS.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) If every speaker prepended, the path would grow by one per router inside the AS and the attribute would no longer describe AS-level hops.</li><li>(C) Nothing duplicates an AS number deliberately; that is only done as a traffic-engineering trick (AS path prepending) when exporting.</li><li>(D) AS_PATH is carried on iBGP routes as well — it is one of the mandatory attributes for any route.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing iBGP is a separate routing protocol. It is the same protocol with a different session type; the differences are the no-prepend rule, the no-re-advertise rule and the unchanged NEXT_HOP.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> eBGP prepends the sender's AS and sets NEXT_HOP to itself; iBGP does neither.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q11", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"11\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"easy\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 11</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag easy\">easy</span><span class=\"tag src\">arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A host broadcasts an ARP request for its <i>own</i> IP address — a gratuitous ARP. What is the usual purpose?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">To announce its own IP-to-MAC binding to the LAN and to detect whether another host is already using the same IP address.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">To ask the default gateway for the MAC address of a remote server.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">To translate a private address into a public one at the router.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"11\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q11\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">To obtain an IP address, subnet mask and default gateway from a server.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["To announce its own IP-to-MAC binding to the LAN and to detect whether another host is already using the same IP address.", "To ask the default gateway for the MAC address of a remote server.", "To translate a private address into a public one at the router.", "To obtain an IP address, subnet mask and default gateway from a server."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A gratuitous ARP is an unsolicited request or reply that states the sender's own binding. It serves two purposes: it refreshes neighbours' caches after a change (for example a NIC swap or a failover), and it acts as a duplicate-address test.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The host broadcasts “who has my own address?”.</li><li>If another host replies, two machines claim the same IP address and the sender can report a conflict.</li><li>If nobody replies, every station that receives the request updates its ARP cache with the sender's binding.</li><li>That is why gratuitous ARP is used by clustering and virtualisation software when a virtual machine moves to a new physical host: it forces the switch and the peers to relearn the MAC.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — announce the binding and detect a duplicate address.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) A gateway lookup uses an ordinary ARP request for the gateway's address, not the sender's own.</li><li>(C) Address translation is NAT's job, at the router, using its translation table.</li><li>(D) Obtaining an address is DHCP, not ARP.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading a gratuitous ARP as an error. It is a normal, deliberate announcement; ARP itself has no authentication, which is exactly why ARP spoofing works.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ordinary ARP asks a question about somebody else; gratuitous ARP makes a statement about yourself.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q12", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"12\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 12</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about distance-vector routing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"12\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q12\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">A router knows only its own links and the distance vectors its neighbours advertise; it never sees the full topology.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"12\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q12\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">The update rule is the Bellman-Ford equation: D(x, y) = min over neighbours v of { c(x, v) + D(v, y) }.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"12\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q12\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">A router normally learns of a link failure from a changed or missing advertisement from a neighbour.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"12\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q12\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Triggered updates remove the count-to-infinity problem completely.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["A router knows only its own links and the distance vectors its neighbours advertise; it never sees the full topology.", "The update rule is the Bellman-Ford equation: D(x, y) = min over neighbours v of { c(x, v) + D(v, y) }.", "A router normally learns of a link failure from a changed or missing advertisement from a neighbour.", "Triggered updates remove the count-to-infinity problem completely."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Distance vector is distributed Bellman-Ford. The state at each node is a vector of distances, one entry per destination, and the only input from outside is the neighbours' vectors.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. That partial view is the defining limitation and the reason path information is absent.</li><li>(B) True. Relaxation over all neighbours is exactly the Bellman-Ford update.</li><li>(C) True. There is no failure notification; a router infers failure from a neighbour that stops advertising the route or advertises it as infinity.</li><li>(D) False. Triggered updates send the news sooner, which shortens the loop but does not remove it: the counting still happens, just faster.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) overstates the fix. Only path information (BGP) or a topology database (link state) removes the problem; triggered updates merely accelerate convergence.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing “faster updates = no loops”. A loop is a structural consequence of path ignorance, not of timer length.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> DV = Bellman-Ford on neighbour vectors. Its defects all trace back to the missing path.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q13", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"13\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 13</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">In OSPF, the cost of an interface is normally computed as</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">reference bandwidth ÷ interface bandwidth, with a minimum of 1.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">1 divided by the number of hops to the destination.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">a constant 1 on every link, whatever the link speed.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"13\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q13\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">the propagation delay of the medium, in microseconds.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["reference bandwidth ÷ interface bandwidth, with a minimum of 1.", "1 divided by the number of hops to the destination.", "a constant 1 on every link, whatever the link speed.", "the propagation delay of the medium, in microseconds."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> OSPF cost is inversely proportional to bandwidth, so a faster link is cheaper. The default reference bandwidth is 100 Mbps, which gives cost 1 to a 100 Mbps link, 10 to 10 Mbps, and 100 to 1 Mbps.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Cost = reference bandwidth / interface bandwidth, rounded up, and never less than 1.</li><li>With the default 100 Mbps reference: 100 Mbps → 1, 10 Mbps → 10, 1 Gbps → 1 (floored at the minimum).</li><li>Because 1 Gbps and 10 Gbps both give 1, modern networks raise the reference bandwidth (for example to 10 Gbps or 100 Gbps) so that faster links stay distinguishable.</li><li>Dijkstra then minimises the sum of these costs, so the chosen path is the highest-bandwidth path.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — cost = reference bandwidth / interface bandwidth.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) OSPF is not a hop-count protocol; hop count is RIP's metric.</li><li>(C) A constant cost of 1 would make OSPF a hop-count protocol and ignore link speed entirely.</li><li>(D) Delay can be configured manually as a cost, but it is not the default formula.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the minimum of 1. Without it, any link faster than the reference bandwidth would compute a cost below 1 and distort the shortest-path tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> OSPF cost is a bandwidth metric. If every link in the topology is faster than the reference bandwidth, every cost is 1 and OSPF degenerates into hop count — a classic exam trap.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q14", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "bgp-and-path-vector", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"14\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"bgp-and-path-vector\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 14</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">bgp-and-path-vector</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An autonomous system has 6 BGP routers, fully meshed with iBGP sessions, and no route reflector is deployed.<br/>How many iBGP sessions exist in total?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 14\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"14\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer number of sessions.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "15", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> iBGP does not re-advertise a route learned from one iBGP peer to another, so a full mesh is required: every pair of routers inside the AS must have a direct session.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A full mesh on <code class=\"mm\">n</code> routers needs one session per unordered pair.</li><li>Number of pairs = n(n − 1) / 2.</li><li>With n = 6: 6 × 5 / 2 = 15.</li><li>So 15 iBGP sessions are needed, which is why large ASes use route reflectors or confederations instead.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 15 iBGP sessions.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting 30 by multiplying 6 × 5 and forgetting to halve. A session between R1 and R2 is the same session seen from both ends.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Full mesh = n(n − 1)/2. Each added router costs n − 1 new sessions, which is why the mesh scales quadratically.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q15", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"15\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 15</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A home router performs NAPT. The internal host 192.168.0.5:5000 sends a UDP datagram to 8.8.8.8:53, and the router's translation table holds 192.168.0.5:5000 ↔ 203.0.113.7:62000. In the datagram that leaves the router, the source IP address and source port are</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">203.0.113.7 and 62000</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.0.5 and 5000</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">203.0.113.7 and 5000</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"15\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q15\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">192.168.0.5 and 62000</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["203.0.113.7 and 62000", "192.168.0.5 and 5000", "203.0.113.7 and 5000", "192.168.0.5 and 62000"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> NAPT (NAT with port translation, also called masquerading) rewrites both the source address and the source port on the way out, so many internal hosts can share one public address and be told apart by port.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The outbound datagram arrives with source 192.168.0.5:5000.</li><li>The router looks up its translation table and finds the mapping to 203.0.113.7:62000.</li><li>It rewrites the source address and port to that public pair, updates the IP checksum and the UDP checksum, and forwards the datagram.</li><li>When the reply arrives addressed to 203.0.113.7:62000, the reverse lookup restores 192.168.0.5:5000 and the internal host receives it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 203.0.113.7 and 62000.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) leaves the private address visible, so the packet would be dropped upstream: private addresses are not routable on the Internet.</li><li>(C) translates the address but not the port; two internal hosts using port 5000 could then not be told apart.</li><li>(D) keeps the private address while changing the port, which is meaningless — the port is only useful once the address is the shared public one.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking only the IP address changes. With NAPT the port is the discriminator, which is why the technique is called <i>network address and port</i> translation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Outbound: rewrite source address and source port. Inbound reply: reverse the mapping. Both checksums are recomputed.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q16", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"16\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 16</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Split horizon without poisoned reverse does not prevent count-to-infinity in every topology. Why not?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"16\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q16\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">It only suppresses advertising a route back to the neighbour that supplied it, so a router can still adopt a worse route learned from a third neighbour, and that worse route keeps the failed route alive.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"16\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q16\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">It works only when the metric is a 32-bit integer rather than a small hop count.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"16\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q16\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">It requires flooding the routing table, which distance-vector protocols cannot do.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"16\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q16\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">It applies only to link-state protocols, so it has no effect in distance-vector routing.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["It only suppresses advertising a route back to the neighbour that supplied it, so a router can still adopt a worse route learned from a third neighbour, and that worse route keeps the failed route alive.", "It works only when the metric is a 32-bit integer rather than a small hop count.", "It requires flooding the routing table, which distance-vector protocols cannot do.", "It applies only to link-state protocols, so it has no effect in distance-vector routing."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Split horizon is a local rule about one neighbour. In a topology with a cycle of three or more routers, a router can learn an alternative — but wrong — route to the same destination through a different neighbour, and no local rule prevents that.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Consider A—B—C—A in a triangle, with network N attached to A. A's link to N fails.</li><li>Split horizon stops B telling A about N (B learned N from A) and stops C telling A about N.</li><li>But B can learn N from C, and C can learn N from B. Neither learned it from the other, so neither is blocked by split horizon.</li><li>Their mutual advertisements carry a distance that grows round by round, and A then learns the inflated distance back through B or C.</li><li>Only path information, a hold-down, or a full topology view breaks this cycle.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — a third neighbour can supply a stale route that split horizon cannot block.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) The metric width changes only how long the count takes, not whether it happens.</li><li>(C) Flooding is not part of distance-vector routing at all, so this is not what limits split horizon.</li><li>(D) Split horizon is a distance-vector technique; it has no meaning in link-state routing, which needs no such rule.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing split horizon makes RIP loop-free. It removes two-node loops only; RIP still needs hold-down timers and triggered updates, and even then large loops can form.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Split horizon: cures 2-router loops. Poisoned reverse: cures 2-router loops faster. Three or more routers: only path vectors or link state truly fix it.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q17", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"17\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 17</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">ICMP messages are carried inside IP datagrams with protocol number 1, and are used by ping and traceroute.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">A DHCP server listens on UDP port 67 and a DHCP client on UDP port 68.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">An ARP request is broadcast at the link layer, while an ARP reply is normally unicast back to the requester.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"17\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q17\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">A NAT router must rewrite the IP header checksum, but it never needs to touch the transport-layer checksum.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["ICMP messages are carried inside IP datagrams with protocol number 1, and are used by ping and traceroute.", "A DHCP server listens on UDP port 67 and a DHCP client on UDP port 68.", "An ARP request is broadcast at the link layer, while an ARP reply is normally unicast back to the requester.", "A NAT router must rewrite the IP header checksum, but it never needs to touch the transport-layer checksum."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> ICMP, DHCP and ARP are three helper protocols at three different layers, and each has a fixed encapsulation that exam questions test directly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. ICMP is an IP payload with protocol number 1; ping uses echo request/reply and traceroute uses TTL expiry messages.</li><li>(B) True. Server 67, client 68, both UDP — the client has no address yet, so broadcast is used at first.</li><li>(C) True. The request must reach everyone because the target's MAC is unknown; the reply goes straight back to the address that asked.</li><li>(D) False. Because NAPT changes the source port (or the destination port on the way back), the TCP or UDP checksum, which covers the pseudo-header with the IP addresses and the port numbers, must also be recomputed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) is the classic NAT trap: address rewriting alone would need only the IP checksum, but port rewriting breaks the transport checksum too.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the transport checksum excludes the IP addresses. It includes a pseudo-header built from them, which is exactly why NAT has to recompute it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> ICMP = protocol 1 inside IP; DHCP = UDP 67/68; ARP = broadcast request, unicast reply; NAT = both checksums.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q18", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"18\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 18</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Router A has two neighbours: B, on a link of cost 2, and C, on a link of cost 1. B advertises a distance of 5 to network N2, and C advertises a distance of 2 to the same network.<br/>Using the Bellman-Ford rule, what distance does A compute for N2?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 18\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"18\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer distance.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A distance-vector router computes each entry by relaxing over all neighbours: the cost through a neighbour is the link cost plus that neighbour's advertised distance, and the minimum wins.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Through B: 2 + 5 = 7.</li><li>Through C: 1 + 2 = 3.</li><li>A takes the minimum: min(7, 3) = 3, with next hop C.</li><li>The entry stored is (destination N2, cost 3, next hop C).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3, reached through neighbour C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the two advertised distances (5 + 2) or choosing the neighbour with the smaller advertised distance alone (C at 2, ignoring the link cost). The rule is link cost plus advertised distance, per neighbour, then minimum.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Bellman-Ford in one line: for each neighbour, add your link cost to its advertised distance; keep the smallest.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q19", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"19\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 19</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Six OSPF routers share one broadcast LAN, and the network has elected one designated router (DR) and one backup designated router (BDR). How many full adjacencies — complete database exchanges — are formed on that LAN?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">9</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">15</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">10</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"19\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q19\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">5</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["9", "15", "10", "5"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> On a multi-access LAN, OSPF does not build a full mesh of adjacencies. Every non-DR router forms a full adjacency with the DR and with the BDR only; the DR and BDR also pair with each other. The rest of the routers stay in the two-way state.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Six routers = 1 DR + 1 BDR + 4 DROthers.</li><li>DR–BDR: 1 adjacency.</li><li>DR with each of the 4 DROthers: 4 adjacencies.</li><li>BDR with each of the 4 DROthers: 4 adjacencies.</li><li>Total = 1 + 4 + 4 = 9, which is 2(n − 2) + 1 for n = 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 9 full adjacencies.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) 15 is the full mesh n(n − 1)/2 = 15, the count you would get without a DR/BDR — exactly the waste OSPF avoids.</li><li>(C) 10 is 2(n − 2) + 2, an off-by-one in the DR–BDR pair.</li><li>(D) 5 counts only the DR's adjacencies and forgets the BDR's.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that all six routers become fully adjacent, or that the DR alone is the hub. Both the DR and the BDR are hubs, which is why the count is 2(n − 2) + 1 and not n − 1.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Broadcast LAN: 2(n − 2) + 1 adjacencies. Full mesh: n(n − 1)/2. The DR/BDR design exists to turn a quadratic adjacency count into a linear one.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q20", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "bgp-and-path-vector", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"20\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"bgp-and-path-vector\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 20</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">bgp-and-path-vector</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A router in AS 100 receives an eBGP update whose AS_PATH is “200 300”. It advertises the route to a peer in AS 400. What AS_PATH does the peer in AS 400 receive?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"20\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q20\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">100 200 300</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"20\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q20\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">200 300</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"20\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q20\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">400 100 200 300</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"20\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q20\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">300 200 100</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["100 200 300", "200 300", "400 100 200 300", "300 200 100"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When a BGP speaker exports a route to an <i>external</i> peer it prepends its own AS number to AS_PATH, so that the path records every AS the route has crossed and any receiver can detect a loop by finding its own AS in the list.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The router in AS 100 holds the route with AS_PATH “200 300”.</li><li>It exports the route over an eBGP session, so it prepends 100.</li><li>The advertised AS_PATH becomes “100 200 300”.</li><li>The peer in AS 400 adds 400 only when it re-exports the route to a further AS; AS 400 does not appear in what it received.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — 100 200 300.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) omits the prepend that eBGP requires.</li><li>(C) prepends the <i>receiving</i> AS, which never appears until that AS exports the route onwards.</li><li>(D) reverses the order; AS_PATH is ordered from the nearest AS to the origin.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the receiver's AS is added on arrival. Nothing is prepended on receipt; the sender prepends on export.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> AS_PATH grows at each AS boundary, left to right from the receiver towards the origin. A route with your own AS in the path is discarded as a loop.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q21", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"21\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 21</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">arp-rarp-icmp-dhcp-nat</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following are genuine limitations or side effects of traditional NAT/NAPT? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">An internal host cannot be reached from the public Internet unless a static mapping or a port-forwarding rule exists.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Protocols that carry IP addresses inside the payload, such as active-mode FTP or SIP, need an application-level gateway.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">NAT breaks the end-to-end addressability that the original Internet architecture assumed.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"21\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q21\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">NAT guarantees that transport-layer checksums never have to be recomputed.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["An internal host cannot be reached from the public Internet unless a static mapping or a port-forwarding rule exists.", "Protocols that carry IP addresses inside the payload, such as active-mode FTP or SIP, need an application-level gateway.", "NAT breaks the end-to-end addressability that the original Internet architecture assumed.", "NAT guarantees that transport-layer checksums never have to be recomputed."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> NAT was introduced to stretch IPv4 address space, and it pays for that with the end-to-end principle: the address a host believes it has is no longer the address the rest of the Internet can use to reach it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Without a static NAT entry or a forwarded port, an unsolicited inbound packet has no translation entry to match, so it is dropped.</li><li>(B) True. Active-mode FTP sends the client's IP and port inside the FTP payload, and SIP embeds addresses in its headers; NAT cannot rewrite payloads it does not understand, so an ALG must.</li><li>(C) True. End-to-end addressing and the ability to accept inbound connections are lost; NAT traversal mechanisms such as STUN and hole punching exist precisely because of this.</li><li>(D) False. NAPT changes port numbers, and the TCP/UDP checksum covers the pseudo-header that contains them, so the checksum must be recomputed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(D) states the opposite of what NAT must do; checksum recomputation is one of its costs.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing NAT is transparent. It is transparent only for simple outbound client-server flows; anything that embeds addresses, expects inbound connections or relies on IP-address identity is affected.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> NAT trades address space for end-to-end transparency, and it needs an ALG wherever the application carries addresses itself.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q22", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"22\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"medium\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 22</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag medium\">medium</span><span class=\"tag src\">distance-vector-routing-count-to-infinity</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">In RIP, which timer causes a route to be advertised with a metric of 16 — unreachable — before the entry is finally removed from the table?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"22\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q22\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">The timeout (invalid) timer, which expires after 180 seconds without an update for that route.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"22\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q22\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">The update timer, which fires every 30 seconds and sends the whole table.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"22\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q22\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">The hold-down timer, which is a fixed 60 seconds for every route.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"22\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q22\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">The flush (garbage-collection) timer, which runs for 120 seconds after the route becomes invalid.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["The timeout (invalid) timer, which expires after 180 seconds without an update for that route.", "The update timer, which fires every 30 seconds and sends the whole table.", "The hold-down timer, which is a fixed 60 seconds for every route.", "The flush (garbage-collection) timer, which runs for 120 seconds after the route becomes invalid."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> RIP keeps four timers per route. The <i>update</i> timer sends the table every 30 s; the <i>timeout</i> (invalid) timer marks the route unreachable after 180 s of silence; the <i>hold-down</i> timer keeps it unreachable for a further period so that stale good news cannot revive it; the <i>flush</i> timer removes the entry after 240 s in total.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>No update mentioning the route arrives for 180 s.</li><li>The timeout timer expires, the metric is set to 16 and the route is advertised as unreachable; it is now in hold-down.</li><li>At 240 s the flush timer expires and the entry is deleted from the table.</li><li>So the timer that <i>causes the advertisement of 16</i> is the 180-second timeout timer.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — the 180-second timeout (invalid) timer.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) The update timer only triggers periodic advertisements; it does not invalidate anything.</li><li>(C) Hold-down begins <i>after</i> the route is already unreachable; its length is also not a universal 60 s.</li><li>(D) The flush timer deletes the entry; deletion is the end of the process, not the advertisement of 16.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing hold-down with timeout. Timeout decides that a route is dead; hold-down decides how long the news “it is dead” is protected from being overwritten by a worse rumour.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> RIP timers: update 30 s, timeout 180 s, flush 240 s, hold-down 180 s in the classic specification. Infinity is 16.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q23", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"23\" data-type=\"NAT\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 23</span><span class=\"tag nat\">NAT</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">An OSPF area has these link costs: A–B 4, A–C 2, B–C 1, B–D 5, C–D 8, C–E 10, D–E 2, D–F 6, E–F 3.<br/>What is the total cost of the least-cost path from A to F?</p><div class=\"natrow\"><label style=\"font-size:13.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Your answer:</label><input aria-label=\"Numerical answer for question 23\" autocomplete=\"off\" class=\"natinput\" data-n=\"23\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"numeric value\" type=\"text\"/></div><div class=\"hintline\">Answer format: an integer cost.</div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": [], "answer_display": "13", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Dijkstra settles nodes in increasing order of distance, and each settled node relaxes its own links once. The cost of the best path to F is the value F holds when it is settled.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Settle A (0). Relax: B = 4, C = 2.</li><li>Settle C (2). Relax through C: B = min(4, 2+1) = 3; D = 2+8 = 10; E = 2+10 = 12.</li><li>Settle B (3). Relax through B: D = min(10, 3+5) = 8.</li><li>Settle D (8). Relax through D: E = min(12, 8+2) = 10; F = 8+6 = 14.</li><li>Settle E (10). Relax through E: F = min(14, 10+3) = 13.</li><li>Settle F (13). The path is A→C→B→D→E→F with cost 2 + 1 + 5 + 2 + 3 = 13.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 13.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at the first path found, for example A→C→D→F at 2 + 8 + 6 = 16, or assuming the fewest-hop route A→B→D→F at 4 + 5 + 6 = 15 is optimal. Both are beaten by a five-link path, because cost, not hop count, is what is minimised.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Relax every settled node exactly once and keep a table of tentative distances; the cheapest route is often the one with more hops.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q24", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "bgp-and-path-vector", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"24\" data-type=\"MCQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"bgp-and-path-vector\" data-source=\"STANDARD_TEXTBOOK\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 24</span><span class=\"tag mcq\">MCQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">bgp-and-path-vector</span><span class=\"tag src\">standard textbook</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 1</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">A BGP router holds two routes to 10.10.0.0/16. Both have the same WEIGHT, the same LOCAL_PREF and the same ORIGIN (IGP). Route P has AS_PATH length 3 and MED 100; route Q has AS_PATH length 3 and MED 50. Which route is selected, and by which step of the decision process?</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"radio\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Route Q, because MED is the next attribute compared and the lower MED wins.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"radio\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">Route P, because a higher MED means a shorter internal path.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"radio\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">Neither is preferred; the router installs both and load-balances across them by default.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"24\" data-typ=\"MCQ\" name=\"q24\" type=\"radio\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">Route P, because the larger MED is preferred once the AS_PATH lengths are equal.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["Route Q, because MED is the next attribute compared and the lower MED wins.", "Route P, because a higher MED means a shorter internal path.", "Neither is preferred; the router installs both and load-balances across them by default.", "Route P, because the larger MED is preferred once the AS_PATH lengths are equal."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> BGP breaks ties lexicographically. The usual order is: highest WEIGHT, then highest LOCAL_PREF, then locally originated, then shortest AS_PATH, then lowest ORIGIN code, then lowest MED, then eBGP over iBGP, then lowest IGP metric to NEXT_HOP, then lowest router ID.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>WEIGHT equal — no decision.</li><li>LOCAL_PREF equal — no decision.</li><li>Neither route is locally originated.</li><li>AS_PATH length 3 for both — no decision.</li><li>ORIGIN is IGP for both — no decision.</li><li>MED decides, and the <i>lower</i> MED is preferred, so route Q with MED 50 wins.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — route Q, at the MED comparison.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) and (D) reverse the MED rule; lower is better, because MED is a hint that the neighbouring AS prefers that entry point.</li><li>(C) BGP installs one best route per prefix by default; multipath is an optional feature, not the default.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming BGP picks the shortest path. It picks the best route by a fixed policy sequence, and administrative preference (LOCAL_PREF, WEIGHT) outranks path length.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Memorise the order as a chain: Weight, LocalPref, originated, AS_PATH, Origin, MED, eBGP, IGP metric, router ID.</p>"}, {"id": "CN-U4-Q25", "subject": "computer-networks", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "routing-protocols", "subtopic": "link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "<div class=\"qcard hidden\" data-n=\"25\" data-type=\"MSQ\" data-difficulty=\"hard\" data-subject=\"computer-networks\" data-unit=\"routing-protocols\" data-unit-number=\"4\" data-subtopic=\"link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra\" data-source=\"ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS\"><div class=\"card\"><div class=\"qhead\"><span class=\"qno\">Question 25</span><span class=\"tag msq\">MSQ</span><span class=\"tag hard\">hard</span><span class=\"tag src\">link-state-routing-ospf-dijkstra</span><span class=\"tag src\">original synthesis</span><span style=\"margin-left:auto;font-size:12.5px;color:#5b6b7a\">Marks: 2</span></div><div class=\"qbody\"><p class=\"qtext\">Considering distance-vector and link-state routing together, which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)</p><div class=\"opts\"><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"0\"><input data-n=\"25\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q25\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"0\"/><span class=\"lbl\">A</span><span class=\"txt\">Split horizon with poisoned reverse removes two-router counting loops but not loops that involve three or more routers.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"1\"><input data-n=\"25\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q25\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"1\"/><span class=\"lbl\">B</span><span class=\"txt\">A link-state protocol can also count to infinity, because it too exchanges periodic distance vectors.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"2\"><input data-n=\"25\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q25\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"2\"/><span class=\"lbl\">C</span><span class=\"txt\">In distance-vector routing a router may have to accept a worse distance for a destination when the increase is reported by the neighbour that is its current next hop.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label><label class=\"opt\" data-i=\"3\"><input data-n=\"25\" data-typ=\"MSQ\" name=\"q25\" type=\"checkbox\" value=\"3\"/><span class=\"lbl\">D</span><span class=\"txt\">While flooding is still in progress, two link-state routers can hold different views of the topology and so compute inconsistent forwarding tables for a short time.</span><span class=\"mark\"></span></label></div><div class=\"qfoot\"><button class=\"btn warn sm\" id=\"btnMark\">Mark for review</button><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnClear\">Clear response</button><span style=\"flex:1\"></span><button class=\"btn sm\" id=\"btnPrev\">&larr; Previous</button><button class=\"btn primary sm\" id=\"btnNext\">Save &amp; Next &rarr;</button></div><div class=\"sol hidden\"></div></div></div></div>", "options": ["Split horizon with poisoned reverse removes two-router counting loops but not loops that involve three or more routers.", "A link-state protocol can also count to infinity, because it too exchanges periodic distance vectors.", "In distance-vector routing a router may have to accept a worse distance for a destination when the increase is reported by the neighbour that is its current next hop.", "While flooding is still in progress, two link-state routers can hold different views of the topology and so compute inconsistent forwarding tables for a short time."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two designs fail in different ways. Distance vector fails through path ignorance and can count upwards. Link state cannot count to infinity — it has no distances to count — but it can be temporarily inconsistent while the flooded database is still converging.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Poisoned reverse blocks only the neighbour that supplied the route; a third router can still offer a stale alternative, so larger loops survive.</li><li>(B) False. Link-state routers exchange link descriptions, not distance vectors, and each recomputes from a full topology; the metric never climbs towards infinity.</li><li>(C) True. This is the mechanism behind counting: if the current next hop reports a larger distance, the router has no better option and adopts the larger value, which is exactly the count.</li><li>(D) True. Convergence is not instantaneous. During flooding, routers along the flooding frontier still hold the old topology and can forward on a loop until the last LSA arrives.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Why the other options are wrong.</b></p><ul><li>(B) attributes a distance-vector defect to a protocol that does not exchange distance vectors.</li></ul><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing link-state routing is instantly consistent. It converges quickly, but the transient window is real — and that window is where micro-loops form after a topology change.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Distance vector: converges slowly, can count to infinity. Link state: converges fast, but transiently inconsistent. Neither is instantaneous.</p>"}];